-- GENERERAD av scripts/generate-charter-data.mjs, ändra inte för hand.
-- 136 flygplatser, 96 resmål, 95 foton, 500 tidtabeller. Priser i hela kronor (1 EUR = 10 kr).






- **Car or taxi:** about 20 minutes from Ras Al Khaimah Airport or an hour from Dubai.', 'The village is open to visitors, and heritage events take place in the cooler months. Summer is extremely hot. Respect the mosque, and do not enter fenced-off ruins, which can be unsafe.'),
  ('alcatraz', 'Alcatraz Island lies in the middle of San Francisco Bay, about 2 km from the city''s waterfront. It served as a military fort and a military prison before becoming a maximum-security federal penitentiary in 1934, intended for prisoners who had caused trouble elsewhere. Its notorious inmates included Al Capone, George "Machine Gun" Kelly and Robert Stroud, the "Birdman of Alcatraz". The cold water and strong currents made escape almost impossible; in the most famous attempt, in 1962, three men disappeared and were never found. The prison closed in 1963 because it had become too expensive to run. From 1969 to 1971 the island was occupied by Native American activists, whose painted messages can still be seen. Today it is part of a national recreation area visited by more than a million people a year. In 2025 the US government announced plans to reopen Alcatraz as a prison, so its future as a visitor site is uncertain.', '- **Train and walk:** from San Francisco International Airport (SFO), about 25 km away, take BART to Embarcadero, about 35 minutes, then walk about 15 minutes to Pier 33.
- **Ferry:** the official ferry from Pier 33 takes about 15 minutes to the island.', 'Tickets often sell out weeks in advance in summer; book through the official ferry operator. Allow about three hours including the ferry. The cellhouse audio tour is included. Wear layers, as it is often cold and windy. Check the current status before planning a visit, given the plans to reopen the prison.'),
  ('ashio-copper-mine', 'The Ashio Copper Mine lies in a mountain valley in Nikkō, in Tochigi Prefecture north of Tokyo. Copper was mined here from the seventeenth century, and after the Furukawa company took over in 1877 it became the largest copper mine in Japan, supplying a country that was industrialising fast. The growth came at a terrible cost: smoke from the smelter killed the forests on the surrounding mountains, and waste in the Watarase River poisoned fields and fish downstream, causing what is considered Japan''s first major industrial pollution disaster. The politician Tanaka Shōzō led a famous campaign for the victims. Mining ended in 1973. Today visitors can ride a small train into part of the old tunnels, where life-size figures show how miners worked through the centuries, and see the remains of the smelter and miners'' housing in the town. Replanting has slowly brought the bare mountainsides back to green.', '- **Plane:** Tokyo Haneda Airport (HND) is about 170 km away.
- **Train:** travel via Tokyo to Kiryū, about two hours, then take the Watarase Keikoku Railway up the valley to Tsūdō station, about 1½ hours.', 'The mine tour is open daily with an entry fee and takes about an hour; it is cool inside the tunnels. The smelter ruins and the old town can be explored on foot. A visit combines well with the temples of Nikkō.'),
  ('bannack', 'Bannack is a ghost town in south-western Montana, founded after gold was discovered on Grasshopper Creek in 1862. Within a year it had several thousand residents, and in 1864 it briefly became the first capital of the new Montana Territory. Its early years were violent: the town''s sheriff, Henry Plummer, was accused of leading a gang of road agents and was hanged by vigilantes in 1864. Mining continued on a smaller scale for decades, but the population gradually dwindled, and the last residents left in the 1970s. Bannack is now a state park, with around sixty log, brick and frame buildings along the main street, including the old courthouse that became the Hotel Meade, a Masonic lodge and schoolhouse, saloons and the jail. Many of the buildings can be entered, giving a vivid picture of life in a frontier mining town.', '- **Plane:** the nearest airport is Butte (BTM), about 120 km away. Bozeman (BZN), about 200 km away, has more flights.
- **Car:** from Butte, allow about 1½ hours via Dillon. The last part is a gravel road.', 'The park is open all year with an entry fee, and many buildings can be entered. In July, a living-history festival brings the town back to life. Services are limited, so bring food and water.'),
  ('bannerman-castle', 'Bannerman Castle stands on Pollepel Island, a small rocky island in the Hudson River about 90 km north of New York City. In 1900 Francis Bannerman VI, a dealer who had grown rich buying up military surplus after the Spanish-American War, bought the island to store his huge stock of weapons and ammunition. He designed a castle-like arsenal in the style of the Scottish castles of his ancestors, with towers, turrets and his family name in giant letters on the wall, as an advertisement to passing boats and trains. Bannerman died in 1918, and in 1920 an explosion of stored ammunition damaged the buildings; a fire in 1969 left them a roofless shell. The island is now owned by New York State, and a trust stabilises the ruins and runs tours, theatre performances and events in the restored gardens.', '- **Plane:** the nearest airport is New York Stewart International (SWF), about 20 km away. John F. Kennedy International (JFK), about 110 km away, has many more flights.
- **Train:** Metro-North trains run from Grand Central Terminal to Beacon in about 1½ hours.
- **Boat tour:** guided tours leave by boat from Beacon; kayak tours are also offered.', 'The island can only be visited on guided tours and at events, from about May to October; book ahead. The ruins are fenced off, so follow the paths, which include steep steps.'),
  ('beelitz-heilstatten', 'Beelitz-Heilstätten is a huge hospital complex in the pine forests south-west of Berlin, built from the 1890s as a sanatorium for tuberculosis patients from the city''s working class. Its brick pavilions, spread across a large park, held well over a thousand beds. During the First World War it served as a military hospital, where a young Adolf Hitler was treated in 1916. From 1945 until 1994 the Soviet army ran it as one of its largest military hospitals outside the Soviet Union. After the soldiers left, most of the buildings stood empty, and their crumbling halls, operating theatres and overgrown roofs made Beelitz one of Germany''s most photographed lost places. Parts of the site have since been restored as housing and clinics, while others remain ruins. A treetop walkway now lets visitors look down on the decaying roofs, and guided tours open some of the empty buildings.', '- **Train:** from Berlin Brandenburg Airport (BER), regional trains reach Beelitz-Heilstätten station, right next to the site, in about 1–1½ hours with one change.
- **Car:** about 45–60 minutes from the airport.', 'The treetop walkway and the ruins around it are open to visitors with an entry fee. Guided tours take groups inside some of the empty buildings and should be booked ahead. Entering the ruins on your own is forbidden and dangerous: much of the site is private property and is patrolled.'),
  ('belchite', 'Belchite is a town in Aragon in north-eastern Spain, about 50 km south of Zaragoza. In the summer of 1937 it was the scene of one of the fiercest battles of the Spanish Civil War, fought street by street between Republican and Nationalist forces, and much of the town was destroyed. After the war, the Franco regime decided not to rebuild the old town but to keep its ruins as a war monument, and built a new Belchite next to it, partly with the labour of political prisoners. The inhabitants lived among the ruins for years until the new town was finished, and then moved out. Today the shells of churches, a clock tower and rows of roofless houses stand as a reminder of the war and of the divisions it left behind. The old town is a protected historical site that is visited on guided tours.', '- **Car:** from Zaragoza Airport (ZAZ), about 55 km, allow 50 minutes.
- **Bus:** buses run from Zaragoza to Belchite a few times a day.', 'The old town can only be visited on guided tours, including tours at night; book ahead. Tours last about an hour and a half. The ruins are fragile, so stay with the guide. Belchite is a place of memory, and visitors are asked to behave accordingly.'),
  ('bhangarh-fort', 'Bhangarh is a ruined fortified town in the Aravalli hills of Rajasthan, in the Alwar district between Jaipur and Delhi. It was founded in the late sixteenth century by a Rajput ruler for his son. Inside its walls lie the remains of a bazaar lined with shops, temples, a palace on the hillside, gardens and grand houses, with the hills rising behind. The town declined in the eighteenth century, probably because of famine and the loss of its strategic importance, and was eventually abandoned. Local legends say it was cursed by a sorcerer or a holy man, and Bhangarh is often called the most haunted place in India. The Archaeological Survey of India, which protects the site, forbids entry between sunset and sunrise, a rule that has only added to its reputation. Today it is a popular day trip, with well-preserved temples and atmospheric ruins.', '- **Car:** from Jaipur International Airport (JAI), about 90 km, allow about two hours.', 'The site is open from sunrise to sunset with an entry fee; entry at night is forbidden. Monkeys live among the ruins, so keep food out of sight. Bring water, especially in the hot season.'),
  ('bodie', 'Bodie is a former gold-mining town high in the hills east of the Sierra Nevada in California, close to the Nevada border. Gold was found here in 1859, and after a rich discovery in the late 1870s the town boomed to several thousand people, with dozens of saloons, a Chinatown, churches and a reputation for lawlessness and violence. As the mines declined in the early twentieth century, people drifted away, and by the 1940s Bodie was almost deserted. In 1962 it became Bodie State Historic Park. Around a hundred buildings still stand, kept in a state of "arrested decay": they are repaired just enough to stop them from collapsing, but not restored. Through dusty windows, visitors can see furniture, tools, shelves of goods and schoolbooks left behind, which makes Bodie one of the most authentic ghost towns in the American West.', '- **Car:** from Reno-Tahoe International Airport (RNO), about 190 km, allow 2½–3 hours via US Highway 395 and Bridgeport. The last few kilometres are on an unpaved road. There is no public transport.', 'The park is open all year with an entry fee, but snow can close the road in winter. No food or water is sold, so bring your own. At about 2,500 metres, the weather can be extreme. Local legend says that anyone who takes something from Bodie will have bad luck, and taking anything is forbidden anyway.'),
  ('bokor-hill-station', 'Bokor Hill Station sits on the plateau of Bokor Mountain, about 1,000 metres above sea level in Preah Monivong National Park, near Kampot in southern Cambodia. It was built by the French colonial authorities in the 1920s as a cool retreat from the heat of the lowlands, with a grand hotel and casino, a Catholic church, a post office and villas; hundreds of workers are said to have died building it. The resort was abandoned in the 1940s, briefly revived and abandoned again, and in the 1970s the Khmer Rouge occupied the plateau, followed by years of fighting with Vietnamese forces. For decades the misty, moss-covered ruins were one of Cambodia''s eeriest sights. Since the 2010s a large development has brought a new road, a modern casino resort and new buildings to the mountain, and the old Bokor Palace hotel has been renovated, but the abandoned church and other ruins still stand.', '- **Car or taxi:** from Sihanouk International Airport (KOS), about 110 km, allow about two hours to Kampot, then about an hour up the mountain road.', 'The plateau is open to visitors, and the old church and ruins can be explored freely. Fog and rain are common, and it is much cooler than in the lowlands. Bring a jacket.'),
  ('borne-sulinowo', 'Borne Sulinowo is a small town among lakes and pine forests in West Pomerania, in north-western Poland. It was built in the 1930s as a German military training area and taken over by the Soviet army after the Second World War, becoming one of the largest Soviet garrisons in Poland. For almost fifty years the town did not appear on Polish maps, and Poles were not allowed to enter. When the last Soviet soldiers left in 1992, they handed over an empty town of barracks, officers'' apartment blocks, a hospital and depots. Borne Sulinowo was officially opened, new residents moved in, and today it is a quiet town of about 5,000 people. Many relics of its past remain: Soviet-era buildings, abandoned barracks in the surrounding forest, and a small museum about the town''s hidden history. In summer it hosts a large rally of military vehicles.', '- **Car:** from Szczecin-Goleniów Airport (SZZ), about 140 km, allow two hours. Public transport is limited, with buses from Szczecinek.', 'The town is freely accessible. Some abandoned buildings on its outskirts are fenced off and unsafe; do not enter them. The local museum is open mainly in the summer season.'),
  ('bunkart', 'Bunk''Art is a museum and art space inside a vast nuclear bunker on the outskirts of Tirana, the capital of Albania. It was built in the 1970s for the dictator Enver Hoxha and the leadership of the communist regime, which isolated Albania from the rest of the world and filled the country with hundreds of thousands of small concrete bunkers out of fear of invasion. The five-storey underground complex contains more than a hundred rooms, including offices, living quarters and an assembly hall, designed to keep the government running after a nuclear attack. It was never used for that purpose, and for years after the fall of communism it was forgotten. In 2014 it opened to the public as Bunk''Art, with exhibitions about the history of communist Albania and contemporary art. A second site, Bunk''Art 2, in a smaller bunker in central Tirana, focuses on the secret police.', '- **Taxi:** from Tirana International Airport (TIA), about 20 km, allow 30 minutes to Bunk''Art 1.
- **Bus:** local buses from central Tirana towards the Dajti mountain pass near the entrance.', 'Bunk''Art is open daily with an entry fee; allow about two hours. It is cool underground. The exhibitions describe repression and persecution, and some of the content is distressing.'),
  ('bussana-vecchia', 'Bussana Vecchia is a medieval hill village a few kilometres from Sanremo on the Italian Riviera. On the morning of 23 February 1887, a strong earthquake struck the Ligurian coast and badly damaged the village, killing many of the people gathered in the church for the Ash Wednesday mass. The survivors built a new Bussana closer to the sea and left the old village to decay, and for more than half a century it stood empty. In the late 1950s and the 1960s, artists from Italy and abroad began moving into the ruins, repairing houses without running water or electricity and creating an international artists'' village. Their presence has never been fully legal, since the buildings belong to the state, but the community has stayed. Today visitors wander narrow lanes between roofless walls, studios, galleries and small cafés, with the shell of the old church at the top of the village.', '- **Plane:** Nice Côte d''Azur Airport (NCE) is about 60 km away.
- **Train:** trains run from Nice to Sanremo in about an hour, usually with a change in Ventimiglia; from Sanremo, take a local bus or a taxi, about 15 minutes.
- **Car:** about an hour from the airport.', 'The village is freely accessible. Galleries and shops are open mainly in the tourist season. The lanes are steep, and the ruined church is fenced off. People live here, so respect their privacy and their homes.'),
  ('buzludzha', 'The Buzludzha Monument stands on a peak of the Balkan Mountains in central Bulgaria, about 1,400 metres above sea level. It was built by the Bulgarian Communist Party and opened in 1981 to commemorate the founding of a socialist party at a secret meeting on this spot in 1891. Shaped like a giant concrete saucer with a tall tower beside it, it housed a circular ceremonial hall decorated with huge mosaics of workers, soldiers and communist leaders, while red glass stars on the tower could be seen from far away. After the fall of communism in 1989 the state abandoned the building, which was looted, damaged by the weather and covered in graffiti, and it became one of the world''s most famous abandoned places. Since the late 2010s a foundation has been working to protect the mosaics and save the structure, and the building has been sealed and guarded.', '- **Plane:** the nearest airport is Plovdiv (PDV), about 110 km away, with few flights. Most international travellers fly to Sofia (SOF), about 200 km away.
- **Car:** about 1½ hours from Plovdiv or three hours from Sofia. The last part is a mountain road that may be closed by snow in winter.
- **Day tours:** several operators run day trips from Sofia and Plovdiv.', 'It is not possible to go inside: the building is sealed and guarded around the clock. Visitors can walk around the outside, and the views over the mountains are spectacular. The weather on the peak changes quickly, so bring warm clothing even in summer.'),
  ('chernobyl-pripyat', 'The Chernobyl Exclusion Zone surrounds the nuclear power plant in northern Ukraine where reactor 4 exploded on 26 April 1986, the worst nuclear accident in history. The nearby city of Pripyat, home to about 49,000 people, was evacuated the following day and has never been resettled. Its apartment blocks, schools, hospital, swimming pool and the amusement park with its yellow Ferris wheel, which never opened, have become symbols of the disaster and of urban exploration itself. The zone also contains abandoned villages and the huge Duga radar, a Soviet early-warning antenna. Before 2022, licensed guides took large numbers of visitors on day tours from Kyiv, with radiation checks on the way out. Russian forces occupied the zone in early 2022, and since the war began tourist visits have been suspended. Chernobyl remains the most famous abandoned place in the world, but it cannot currently be visited.', '- **Currently not possible:** tours are suspended, and civil flights to Ukraine are currently suspended too.
- **Before the war:** tours started in Kyiv, about 130 km to the south, with transport to the zone included. Most visitors flew into Kyiv Boryspil International Airport (KBP).', 'The zone is closed to visitors. Entering without a permit has always been illegal, and it is dangerous: parts of the zone are still contaminated, and since 2022 there is also a risk of unexploded ordnance. Follow official Ukrainian sources and your government''s travel advice before making any plans.'),
  ('city-hall-station', 'City Hall station was the southern terminus of New York City''s first subway line, which opened on 27 October 1904. Designed as the showpiece of the new system, it was built on a tight curve beneath City Hall Park, with elegant arched ceilings covered in tiles, brass chandeliers and skylights of coloured glass. As trains grew longer, the curved platform became too short and too dangerous for the new cars, and the station, which had never been busy, closed in 1945. It has been preserved almost unchanged ever since, hidden beneath one of the busiest parts of Manhattan. The New York Transit Museum occasionally runs tours for its members, and anyone can catch a glimpse of the station by staying on board a downtown 6 train as it turns around through the loop after its last stop at Brooklyn Bridge-City Hall.', '- **Plane:** LaGuardia Airport (LGA) is about 15 km away; John F. Kennedy International (JFK) is the main international airport.
- **Metro:** from LaGuardia, take a bus to the subway, then the 4, 5 or 6 train to Brooklyn Bridge-City Hall, about an hour in total.', 'The station can only be entered on New York Transit Museum tours, which are for members and sell out quickly. To glimpse it from the train, stay on a downtown 6 train after the last stop at Brooklyn Bridge-City Hall: it loops through the old station and returns uptown.'),
  ('cockatoo-island', 'Cockatoo Island is the largest island in Sydney Harbour, where the Parramatta and Lane Cove rivers meet, a short ferry ride from the city centre. From 1839 it was a prison for convicts, who were made to cut the sandstone, build the prison buildings and carve huge grain silos into the rock. Later it held a reformatory and an industrial school for girls. In 1857 the Fitzroy Dock was completed, and the island gradually became one of Australia''s largest shipyards, building and repairing ships for the navy through both world wars. The shipyard closed in 1991, leaving behind slipways, workshops, cranes, tunnels and machinery. In 2010 the island became part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site that brings together Australia''s most important convict sites. Today it is managed as a public place, with a campground and holiday houses, and its industrial halls are often used for art exhibitions and events.', '- **Train:** from Sydney Kingsford Smith Airport (SYD), about 15 km, the airport train reaches Circular Quay in about 20 minutes.
- **Ferry:** ferries from Circular Quay reach Cockatoo Island in about 20 minutes.', 'Entry to the island is free. Guided tours and audio guides explain the convict and shipyard history. The island has cafés, a campground and holiday houses for overnight stays. Wear comfortable shoes; the paths and tunnels are uneven.'),
  ('consonno', 'Consonno was once a small farming hamlet on a hillside in the Brianza area near Lecco, north-east of Milan. In 1962 a businessman, Count Mario Bagno, bought the whole village and demolished most of it to build a fantasy resort he called the "city of toys", promising a Las Vegas-style playground for the people of Milan. He added a minaret, a Chinese-style pagoda, a medieval-style gateway, a grand hotel, shops and dance halls, and crowds came at weekends. But in 1976 a landslide destroyed the only access road, visitors stopped coming, and the resort was gradually abandoned. Today the minaret still rises above crumbling arcades covered in graffiti, and the site has become one of Italy''s best-known ghost towns. Local groups organise walks, events and clean-up days, and the hillside is popular with walkers and photographers.', '- **Plane:** Milan Bergamo Airport (BGY) is about 35 km away.
- **Train and walk:** trains run from Milan towards Lecco and stop near Olginate; from there it is a steep walk of about an hour up to Consonno.
- **Car:** about 40 minutes from the airport. The last stretch of road is narrow; park below the village and walk up.', 'The grounds can be walked freely. The buildings are unsafe and should not be entered. Local associations run guided walks and events, especially at weekends. Respect any private property and fenced areas.'),
  ('corregidor', 'Corregidor is a small, tadpole-shaped island at the entrance to Manila Bay in the Philippines, about 50 km west of Manila. Because it controls access to the bay, it was fortified for centuries, and under American rule in the early twentieth century it became Fort Mills, with huge gun batteries, barracks and a network of tunnels. In 1942, after Japan invaded the Philippines, American and Filipino troops held out on Corregidor for months under constant bombardment before surrendering in May; in 1945 American forces retook the island in a fierce battle. Today the island is a memorial and historic site. Visitors tour the shell-scarred ruins of the Mile-Long Barracks, rusting gun emplacements, the Pacific War Memorial and the Malinta Tunnel, which served as headquarters and hospital during the siege and now hosts a light-and-sound show.', '- **Taxi:** from Ninoy Aquino International Airport (MNL), take a taxi to the ferry terminal in Manila, about 30 minutes.
- **Ferry:** day-tour ferries from Manila reach Corregidor in about 1¼ hours.', 'Most visitors come on organised day tours that include the ferry, a guided tram tour of the island and lunch; overnight stays are possible at the island''s hotel. It is hot and humid, so bring sun protection and water. Corregidor is a war memorial, so visit respectfully.'),
  ('craco', 'Craco is a medieval town perched on a steep clay ridge in the Basilicata region of southern Italy, about 50 km from Matera. For centuries its stone houses, churches and palaces climbed the hill towards a Norman tower at the top. From the 1960s a series of landslides, made worse by building work and leaking water pipes, began to tear the ground apart beneath the town. After a flood in the early 1970s and an earthquake in 1980, the remaining residents were moved to a new settlement in the valley below. The old town has been empty ever since, its crumbling buildings silhouetted against the sky. Craco''s dramatic setting has made it a popular film location, used for The Passion of the Christ and a James Bond film, among others. Today it is a protected site that can be visited on guided walks wearing a safety helmet.', '- **Car:** from Bari Karol Wojtyła Airport (BRI), about 120 km, allow 1¾ hours. There is no practical public transport to Craco.', 'Visits are only possible on guided tours, which follow a set route along the edge of the old town; book in advance. Tours last about an hour, and helmets are provided. Most of the town is unstable and closed, so stay with the guide and wear sturdy shoes.'),
  ('denniston', 'Denniston is an old coal-mining settlement on a high, windswept plateau about 600 metres above the sea, near Westport on the West Coast of New Zealand''s South Island. High-quality coal was found here, and mining began in 1879. To bring the coal down from the plateau, engineers built the Denniston Incline, a railway so steep that loaded wagons were lowered by cables, using gravity, while the weight of the descending wagons pulled the empty ones back up. It dropped more than 500 metres and was one of the steepest railways of its kind in the world. For years it was also the only way up for supplies, and even for the miners'' families. In the early 1900s about 1,500 people lived on the plateau in harsh, foggy weather. The incline closed in 1967, and the town has almost vanished. The story inspired Jenny Pattrick''s novel The Denniston Rose.', '- **Car:** from Westport Airport (WSZ), about 25 km, allow 30 minutes; the road climbs up to the plateau from Waimangaroa.
- **Plane:** Westport has flights from Wellington; from Christchurch International Airport (CHC) the drive takes about four hours.', 'The plateau is open and free to visit. Walking tracks with information panels lead to the top of the incline, the old mine entrances and the town site. The weather changes fast, so bring warm and waterproof clothing. Keep away from old mine shafts.'),
  ('dhanushkodi', 'Dhanushkodi lies at the end of a narrow spit of sand on Rameswaram Island, at the south-eastern tip of India in Tamil Nadu, only about 30 km from Sri Lanka. In the early twentieth century it was a busy town with a railway station, a port, a church, a post office and a ferry service to Sri Lanka. On the night of 22 December 1964 a powerful cyclone struck with huge waves, destroying the town and killing many people, including the passengers of a train that was swept into the sea. The government declared the town unfit to live in, and it was never rebuilt. Today the ruins of the church, the railway station and other buildings stand among sand dunes between the Bay of Bengal and the Indian Ocean. In 2017 a new road opened to the tip of the spit, and the area is also an important Hindu pilgrimage place linked to the Ramayana.', '- **Plane:** Madurai Airport (IXM) is about 190 km away.
- **Train or bus:** travel to Rameswaram, about four hours.
- **Bus, jeep or taxi:** continue about 20 km along the new road to Dhanushkodi.', 'The area can be visited during the day; there are no services at night. The sea has strong currents, and swimming is dangerous. Bring water and sun protection, as there is little shade.'),
  ('djupavik', 'Djúpavík is a tiny settlement at the head of a fjord in the Strandir region of Iceland''s Westfjords, one of the most remote corners of the country. In the 1930s, during Iceland''s herring boom, a huge concrete factory was built here to process herring into oil and meal; when it opened in 1935 it was one of the largest concrete buildings in Iceland. Hundreds of workers came every summer, but the herring disappeared from the surrounding waters in the 1940s, and the factory closed in 1954. The village emptied, leaving the factory behind together with the rusting wreck of a ship on the beach. Since the 1980s a family has run a small hotel in the old workers'' quarters and opened the factory for guided tours, exhibitions and art installations. Surrounded by steep mountains and waterfalls, Djúpavík is a quiet, atmospheric place far from the main tourist routes.', '- **Plane:** Gjögur Airport (GJR), about 20 km away, has scheduled flights from Reykjavík a few times a week. Most international travellers arrive at Keflavík (KEF) and drive.
- **Car:** from Reykjavík, allow about five hours. The last stretch is a gravel road that is only reliably passable in summer.', 'The factory is open for guided tours in summer. The hotel offers rooms and meals and is the only service nearby. Weather and road conditions can change quickly, so check them before setting off.'),
  ('doel', 'Doel is a small polder village on the left bank of the river Scheldt, next to the port of Antwerp and in the shadow of a nuclear power station. From the 1960s onwards, plans to expand the port meant that the village would have to make way for new docks, and over the following decades most of its residents were bought out and moved away. Hundreds of houses stood empty, and street artists from all over Europe covered their walls with murals, turning Doel into an open-air gallery and a well-known destination for urban explorers. A small group of residents refused to leave and fought the plans in court for years. More recently the authorities changed course: the village will no longer be demolished, and former residents can buy their old homes back. Doel now lives in an unusual state between ghost town and revival, watched over by its seventeenth-century windmill on the dyke.', '- **Plane:** the nearest airport is Antwerp (ANR), about 25 km away, with few routes. Most international travellers fly to Brussels Airport (BRU), about 60 km away.
- **Car:** about 30 minutes from Antwerp or an hour from Brussels Airport.
- **Bus:** a local bus runs to Doel a few times a day; check the timetable before you travel.', 'The streets of the village are public and free to walk. Some houses are inhabited again, so respect residents'' privacy and do not enter any buildings. The murals change over time as new artists paint over old works. A walk along the Scheldt dyke gives views of the river and the port.'),
  ('eastern-state-penitentiary', 'Eastern State Penitentiary in Philadelphia opened in 1829 and became one of the most famous and influential prisons in the world. Its design, with long cellblocks radiating from a central hub like the spokes of a wheel, was copied by hundreds of prisons. It was built for the "Pennsylvania system" of solitary confinement, in which prisoners lived alone in their cells, meant to reflect on their crimes and become penitent. Over time the system was abandoned, and the prison became overcrowded. Its famous inmates included the gangster Al Capone, whose surprisingly comfortable cell has been recreated. The prison closed in 1971 and stood empty for two decades, its cellblocks crumbling and filling with trees. Since 1994 it has been open as a museum in a state of preserved ruin, with exhibitions about the history of prisons and justice in the United States.', '- **Train:** from Philadelphia International Airport (PHL), about 15 km away, the airport rail line reaches Center City in about 25 minutes; from there, take a bus or walk about 25 minutes to the Fairmount neighbourhood.
- **Taxi:** about 25 minutes from the airport.', 'The museum is open daily with timed tickets, and an audio tour is included. Allow about two hours. A large Halloween event runs in the autumn. Exhibitions also look at mass incarceration in the United States today.'),
  ('elizabeth-bay', 'Elizabeth Bay is an abandoned diamond-mining town on the Atlantic coast of southern Namibia, about 25 km south of Lüderitz. It lies inside the Sperrgebiet, the "forbidden area", a large region that was closed to the public for most of the twentieth century to protect the diamond fields. The town was built in the mid-1920s, when rich diamond deposits were found along this part of the coast, and the mining company built houses, a hospital, workshops and a power station for its workers and their families. The deposits ran out quickly, and the town was abandoned by around 1950. Because Elizabeth Bay lies right on the coast, the strong south winds have blasted the buildings with sand, and many walls have been worn down or have collapsed. Fewer people visit than nearby Kolmanskop, which makes it feel even lonelier.', '- **Plane:** Lüderitz Airport (LUD) is about 25 km away; Hosea Kutako International Airport (WDH) near Windhoek is the main international airport.
- **4x4 tour:** licensed tour operators in Lüderitz drive visitors into the restricted area.', 'Elizabeth Bay can only be visited on licensed tours with a permit. Book a few days ahead and bring your passport, as the operator needs your details to apply. Tours often combine Elizabeth Bay with Kolmanskop. Expect strong wind and blowing sand.'),
  ('ellis-island-hospital', 'Ellis Island in New York Harbor was the main immigration station of the United States from 1892 to 1954, and around 12 million people passed through it. While the main building on the north side of the island is now the famous National Museum of Immigration, the south side holds a far less known complex: the Ellis Island Immigrant Hospital. Opened in 1902 and expanded over the following decades, it had general wards, contagious-disease wards, operating rooms and a morgue, and it treated immigrants who arrived sick, many of them children. It was one of the largest public health hospitals in the country. After the immigration station closed, the 29 hospital buildings were abandoned and slowly decayed. Since 2014 a non-profit organisation has run guided hard-hat tours through the corridors, where peeling walls, empty wards and large photographic artworks of immigrants bring the past back to life.', '- **Plane:** Newark Liberty International Airport (EWR) is about 15 km away; John F. Kennedy International Airport is another option.
- **Train and ferry:** take a train into Manhattan or to Jersey City, then the official ferry from Battery Park or from Liberty State Park, which stops at Liberty Island and Ellis Island.', 'The hospital can only be visited on hard-hat tours of about 90 minutes in small groups; book well ahead, as the tour ticket includes the ferry. There is airport-style security screening before boarding the ferry. Wear comfortable shoes; some areas are dusty and uneven.'),
  ('ferropolis', 'Ferropolis, "the city of iron", lies on a peninsula in a lake that was once the Golpa-Nord open-cast lignite mine near Dessau in Saxony-Anhalt. When mining ended in the early 1990s, five of the giant machines that had dug the coal were saved from the scrapyard and arranged in a semicircle, like the stage of an arena. The largest are more than 30 metres tall and over 100 metres long, and together they weigh thousands of tonnes. The old mine has since been flooded to form the Gremminer See, and the steel giants now tower over a grassy amphitheatre used for music festivals and concerts. Visitors can climb onto some of the excavators, learn how lignite mining reshaped the landscape of eastern Germany, and walk or cycle around the lake.', '- **Train and taxi:** from Leipzig/Halle Airport (LEJ), trains reach Gräfenhainichen via Leipzig or Bitterfeld in about 1–1½ hours. From the station, take a taxi or walk about 30 minutes.
- **Car:** about 45 minutes from the airport.', 'Ferropolis is open to visitors for most of the year, with an entry fee; check opening times, especially in winter. During large festivals the site is closed to day visitors. Guided tours, including climbing tours on the excavators, are offered in season.'),
  ('fordlandia', 'Fordlândia is a town on the banks of the Tapajós River in the Brazilian state of Pará, deep in the Amazon rainforest. In 1928 the American car maker Henry Ford bought a huge area of land here to grow rubber for car tyres, and his company built a complete American town in the jungle, with wooden bungalows, a hospital, a school, a sawmill, a water tower and even a golf course. The project went wrong from the start. The rubber trees, planted close together, were destroyed by leaf blight and insects, and Brazilian workers resented the strict American rules on food, alcohol and daily life, which led to a riot in 1930. Ford moved the plantation downriver to Belterra in the 1930s and sold the land back to Brazil in 1945 at a large loss. Today a few thousand people live among the ruins, and the rusting factory, water tower and empty American houses still stand.', '- **Plane:** Santarém Airport (STM) is the nearest airport, about 300 km downriver, with flights from Belém and Manaus.
- **Boat tour:** tour operators in Santarém arrange trips up the Tapajós; regular riverboats take most of a day.', 'Fordlândia is best visited on an organised tour from Santarém, which covers the boat trip and a local guide. Some old buildings are unsafe, so do not enter them without a guide. Bring insect repellent, sun protection and water.'),
  ('garnet', 'Garnet is a ghost town in the Garnet Range of western Montana, about 1,800 metres up in the forest east of Missoula. It was founded in the 1890s around a gold mine and at its peak, around 1898, had about a thousand residents, with hotels, stores, saloons and a school. As the gold ran out, the town emptied, and a fire in 1912 destroyed many of its buildings; a short revival in the 1930s brought miners back for a few years. Today around thirty buildings survive, including the three-storey Wells Hotel, cabins and a general store, looked after by the Bureau of Land Management together with a preservation group. Because it lies at the end of a mountain road, far from any highway, Garnet has a quiet and authentic atmosphere. In winter the road is closed to cars, and visitors arrive on snowmobiles or skis; two cabins can be rented for overnight stays.', '- **Car:** from Missoula Montana Airport (MSO), about 65 km, allow about 1¼ hours. The last 18 km is a gravel mountain road. In winter the road is closed to cars, and the town is reached by snowmobile or on skis.', 'Garnet is open all year for a small fee, and a visitor centre is staffed in summer. There are no services, so bring water and food. Please treat the old buildings with care.'),
  ('granadilla', 'Granadilla is a small walled village in the north of Extremadura in western Spain, founded in the Middle Ages on a hill above the river Alagón. In the 1950s the Spanish government built the Gabriel y Galán reservoir nearby and expropriated the village, expecting the water to flood its fields and surroundings. The inhabitants were forced to leave by the mid-1960s, although in the end the water rose around the village but never covered it. Granadilla was left almost intact: its castle, stone houses, church and complete town walls now stand on a peninsula in the reservoir. Since the 1980s a government programme has brought school groups and young volunteers to restore the houses, and the village has been declared a historic site. No one lives there permanently, but visitors can walk along the walls and climb the castle tower for views over the water.', '- **Plane:** the nearest airport is Salamanca (SLM), about 110 km away, with few flights. Most international travellers fly to Madrid (MAD), about 280 km away.
- **Car:** about 1¼ hours from Salamanca or three hours from Madrid. There is no public transport to the village.', 'The village is open to visitors during the day; opening hours vary by season, so check before you go. The castle tower has narrow stairs. There are few services, so bring water, especially in summer.'),
  ('grytviken', 'Grytviken is an abandoned whaling station on the island of South Georgia in the South Atlantic, more than 1,400 km east of the Falkland Islands. It was founded in 1904 by the Norwegian whaler Carl Anton Larsen as the first shore-based whaling station in Antarctic waters, and it processed tens of thousands of whales before closing in 1966. Rusting boilers, fuel tanks, slipways and the hulls of old whale catchers still line the shore of King Edward Cove, while elephant seals and fur seals have taken over the beaches. The polar explorer Sir Ernest Shackleton is buried in the small whalers'' cemetery, and the restored Norwegian church still holds occasional services. The South Georgia Museum, in the former station manager''s house, tells the story of whaling and exploration. Grytviken is one of the most remote abandoned industrial sites in the world, and it can only be reached by sea.', '- **Plane and cruise:** there is no airport on South Georgia. Most visitors arrive on expedition cruises that start in Ushuaia, Argentina (USH), often combined with the Falkland Islands and the Antarctic Peninsula.
- **From the Falklands:** some cruises start in the Falkland Islands, which have flights to Mount Pleasant Airport (MPN). The crossing to South Georgia takes about two days.', 'Passengers go ashore by inflatable boat under strict biosecurity rules: boots and clothing are cleaned before landing to protect the island''s wildlife. Keep a safe distance from fur seals, which can be aggressive in the breeding season. The whaling station''s buildings can be seen from outside; the museum, church and cemetery are open during cruise visits. Weather can cancel landings at short notice.'),
  ('haludovo-palace-hotel', 'The Haludovo Palace Hotel stands on the shore of the Adriatic at Malinska on the Croatian island of Krk. It opened in the early 1970s as one of the most luxurious resorts in Yugoslavia, with a striking modernist main building, a "fishing village" of guest bungalows and a casino financed by Bob Guccione, the American founder of Penthouse magazine. The casino soon failed, but the hotel continued to receive guests, many of them from abroad, until the wars of the 1990s, when tourism collapsed and the buildings housed refugees. After a troubled privatisation the complex was left empty, and its glass, concrete and swimming pools slowly decayed. Today the hotel''s ruins are a well-known destination for urban explorers and photographers. Investors have been planning a renovation for years, but the future of the site is still undecided.', '- **Car or taxi:** Rijeka Airport (RJK) is on the island of Krk itself, about 10 km away; allow about 15 minutes.', 'The buildings are closed, unstable and private property, so the hotel can only be seen from outside, from the coastal path and the beach. The bay nearby is good for swimming in summer.'),
  ('hanstholm-bunkers', 'Hanstholm, on the windswept north-west coast of Jutland in Denmark, was one of the most heavily fortified places in northern Europe during the Second World War. The German army built a fortress here as part of the Atlantic Wall, with four giant 38 cm guns that, together with a battery on the Norwegian side, could close the Skagerrak strait to Allied ships. Hundreds of bunkers, gun positions and tunnels were built in the dunes, and local inhabitants had to leave the area. After the war the guns were scrapped, but the concrete remained. Today the Museumscenter Hanstholm occupies one of the gun bunkers, with an underground tour and a narrow-gauge ammunition railway, while many other bunkers lie half buried in the sand along the coast. The surrounding area is part of Thy National Park.', '- **Car:** from Aalborg Airport (AAL), about 95 km, allow 1½ hours.
- **Train and bus:** trains run from Aalborg to Thisted; from there, a bus continues to Hanstholm.', 'The museum charges an entry fee and has seasonal opening hours. Many bunkers in the dunes can be seen freely; some are open, others sealed. Do not enter unstable bunkers, and keep to the paths to protect the dunes.'),
  ('hashima', 'Hashima is a small island about 15 km from the port of Nagasaki in southern Japan. From the late nineteenth century, the Mitsubishi company mined coal from beneath the sea floor here and gradually enlarged the island with sea walls and reclaimed land. To house the miners and their families, it built some of Japan''s first large reinforced-concrete apartment blocks, and at its peak around 1960 more than 5,000 people lived on an island only about 480 metres long, one of the highest population densities ever recorded. Its high walls and blocks give it the silhouette of a warship, hence its nickname Gunkanjima, "Battleship Island". When Japan switched from coal to oil, the mine closed in 1974 and the island was abandoned within weeks. Since 2015 it has been part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site, though the listing is controversial because Korean and Chinese forced labourers worked in the mine during the Second World War.', '- **Plane:** Nagasaki Airport (NGS) is about 40 km away; Fukuoka (FUK) has more international flights.
- **Bus:** the airport bus reaches Nagasaki city in about 45 minutes.
- **Boat tour:** licensed tour boats leave from Nagasaki port and take about 40–50 minutes each way.', 'The island can only be visited on licensed boat tours; book ahead. Visitors may only walk along a short route of walkways at the southern end. Landings are often cancelled when the sea is rough, and the boat then circles the island instead.'),
  ('hotel-del-salto', 'The Hotel del Salto stands on the edge of a cliff facing the Tequendama Falls, where the Bogotá River drops about 150 metres into a deep gorge, some 30 km south-west of Bogotá. The French-style mansion was built in the 1920s as a grand country house for the capital''s wealthy, who came to admire the falls, and it later became a hotel with a restaurant. As Bogotá grew, the river became badly polluted by the city''s sewage and industry, the smell drove the guests away, and the hotel closed in the early 1990s. Standing empty in the mist above the falls, it gained a reputation as one of Colombia''s most haunted buildings, and stories about it are still told today. In 2013 the building was restored and reopened as the Casa Museo del Salto del Tequendama, a museum about the biodiversity and culture of the region and the fight to clean up the river.', '- **Car:** from El Dorado International Airport (BOG), about 35 km, allow 1–1½ hours through Soacha, depending on Bogotá''s traffic.
- **Taxi:** taxis and private drivers can be hired at the airport or in Bogotá for a half-day trip.', 'The museum is open most days with an entry fee; check opening times before you go. The viewpoint over the falls is next to the building. Mist often covers the gorge, so visit early in the day for the best chance of a clear view.'),
  ('houtouwan', 'Houtouwan is a former fishing village on Shengshan Island, part of the Shengsi archipelago at the mouth of the Yangtze River, east of Shanghai. In its heyday the village had more than two thousand residents, who lived in stone houses built on steep slopes above a small bay. From the 1990s, as fishing declined and the island''s remote location made schooling and supplies difficult, families moved to the mainland, and by the early 2000s only a handful of people remained. Over the following years, climbing plants spread over the empty houses until whole streets were covered in green, turning Houtouwan into one of the most photographed abandoned places in China. Since the mid-2010s it has been run as a tourist site with an entry fee, walkways and viewing platforms, and some of the houses in the area have been turned into guesthouses and cafés.', '- **Bus or taxi:** from Shanghai Pudong International Airport (PVG), travel to one of the ferry terminals south of Shanghai, about an hour.
- **Ferry:** take a ferry to the Shengsi islands, about two hours, then a local ferry or bus connection to Shengshan Island.', 'The village is open daily with an entry fee, and visitors follow the walkways; do not enter the houses. Ferries depend on the weather and can be cancelled, and they are busy during holidays, so book ahead and plan to stay overnight.'),
  ('humberstone-santa-laura', 'Humberstone and Santa Laura are two abandoned saltpetre works in the Atacama Desert of northern Chile, about 45 km inland from the port of Iquique. From the late nineteenth century, saltpetre (sodium nitrate) dug from the desert was in huge demand around the world as fertiliser and for explosives, and hundreds of works like these brought great wealth to Chile. Thousands of workers from Chile, Peru and Bolivia lived in company towns in the middle of the desert, with their own culture and traditions. When synthetic nitrate was invented in Germany, the industry collapsed, and both works closed by 1960. Humberstone, named after the British engineer James Humberstone, still has its theatre, church, market, hotel and a swimming pool built from the iron hull of a ship. At Santa Laura, about 1.5 km away, the tall processing plant and its chimney still stand. Together they became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2005.', '- **Car:** from Diego Aracena International Airport (IQQ), south of Iquique, allow about an hour.
- **Bus:** buses from Iquique towards Pozo Almonte pass the site in about 45 minutes; ask the driver to stop at Humberstone.', 'The site is open daily with an entry fee, which also covers Santa Laura. The desert sun is fierce and there is little shade, so bring water, a hat and sun protection, and plan two to three hours. Do not climb on the old machinery.'),
  ('imber', 'Imber is a village in the middle of Salisbury Plain in Wiltshire, in southern England. In December 1943 its inhabitants were told to leave within weeks so that American troops could practise for the invasion of Europe, and they were never allowed to return. The British Army has used the village for training ever since. Most of the original houses have gone, replaced by empty concrete buildings used to rehearse street fighting, but the medieval church of St Giles, the old manor house and a few other buildings still stand. Former residents campaigned for decades to go home, and their story has made Imber a symbol of the villages lost to war. The army opens the roads to Imber on a few days each year, usually around Easter, in August and around Christmas, when services are held in the church and visitors come to see the empty village.', '- **Car:** from Bristol Airport (BRS), about 60 km, allow 1¼ hours. The roads across the training area are only open on the announced open days. From London Heathrow (LHR) allow about two hours.
- **Vintage bus:** on one day each summer, a volunteer-run vintage bus service, the Imberbus, runs to Imber from Warminster.', 'Imber can only be visited on the open days announced by the army; at all other times the area is a live firing range and entry is forbidden. Stay on the roads and marked areas, and never pick up objects from the ground. The church is open on these days.'),
  ('karosta-prison', 'Karosta is a district of the Latvian port city of Liepāja that was built at the end of the nineteenth century as a naval base for the Russian Tsar''s fleet. In the Soviet era it became a closed military town, home to thousands of sailors and their families, and Latvians needed special permission to enter. Its military prison, built around 1900, held disobedient soldiers and sailors under the Tsarist, Latvian, Nazi and Soviet regimes until the 1990s. Today the prison is a museum where guides tell its grim history, and some programmes let visitors experience the harsh treatment of Soviet-era prisoners or even spend a night in a cell. Around it, Karosta still has an atmosphere of decay, with empty apartment blocks, the golden domes of the St Nicholas Naval Cathedral and the broken concrete of the Northern Forts on the beach.', '- **Plane:** Liepāja International Airport (LPX) is about 10 km away but has few flights. Most travellers fly to Riga (RIX), about 220 km away.
- **Bus:** buses run from Riga to Liepāja in about 3½ hours; a local bus or taxi continues to Karosta.', 'Guided tours of the prison run all year, with shorter hours in winter. Some interactive programmes are intense and not suitable for children. The Northern Forts on the beach are free to visit, but the ruins are unstable, especially near the water.'),
  ('kayakoy', 'Kayaköy, once known as Levissi, is a village of several hundred stone houses climbing a hillside in a valley near Fethiye on the south-west coast of Türkiye. For centuries it was home to a Greek Orthodox community of several thousand people, who lived alongside Turkish neighbours in the surrounding area. After the Greco-Turkish War, the 1923 population exchange between Greece and Turkey forced its Greek-speaking inhabitants to leave for Greece. Muslims sent from Greece were meant to replace them, but few settled, and an earthquake in 1957 damaged many of the houses. The village has been empty ever since. Today the roofless houses, churches and chapels stand in rows on the slopes, with views over the pine-covered valley, and the site is protected as an open-air museum. It inspired the setting of Louis de Bernières'' novel Birds Without Wings.', '- **Car:** from Dalaman Airport (DLM), about 60 km, allow an hour via Fethiye.
- **Bus:** minibuses (dolmuş) run from Fethiye to Kayaköy in about 20 minutes.', 'The site is open daily with an entry fee. The paths between the houses are steep and stony, and there is little shade, so mornings and late afternoons are best in summer. Please respect the churches and chapels.'),
  ('kellies-castle', 'Kellie''s Castle stands on a small hill near Batu Gajah in the state of Perak, Malaysia, surrounded by former rubber plantations. It was begun around 1915 by William Kellie Smith, a Scottish planter who had made his fortune in rubber and tin, as a grand home for his family, combining Scottish, Moorish and Indian architectural styles. He brought workers from India to build it, and many of them died during an outbreak of disease; in their memory, he built a Hindu temple nearby. In 1926 Kellie Smith died of pneumonia on a trip to Portugal, and his family returned to Scotland, leaving the mansion unfinished. Since then its red-brick walls, arched windows, rooftop terrace and the shaft of what was planned as one of the first lifts in Malaya have stood empty. Rumours of secret tunnels and ghosts have added to its fame, and it has appeared in films.', '- **Plane:** Sultan Azlan Shah Airport (IPH) in Ipoh is about 20 km away. Kuala Lumpur International Airport (KUL), about 200 km away, has many more flights.
- **Car or taxi:** about 25 minutes from Ipoh. Trains run from Kuala Lumpur to Ipoh in about 2½ hours.', 'The castle is open daily with an entry fee; allow about an hour. Stairs lead up to the roof terrace. It is hot, so bring water.'),
  ('kennecott', 'Kennecott is a former copper-mining camp deep inside Wrangell-St. Elias, the largest national park in the United States, in south-eastern Alaska. Between 1911 and 1938 the Kennecott Copper Corporation mined some of the richest copper ore ever found in the mountains above the Kennicott Glacier and shipped it to the coast on its own railway. A company town grew up around a towering, red-painted concentration mill, with a hospital, a school, a power plant and bunkhouses for hundreds of workers. When the ore ran out, the company left almost overnight, and the buildings were abandoned. The National Park Service bought the site in 1998 and has been stabilising and restoring it ever since. Today the red buildings stand out against the white and grey of the glacier, and visitors can tour the fourteen-storey mill and hike to the old mines.', '- **Car:** from Ted Stevens Anchorage International Airport (ANC), about 500 km, allow seven to eight hours. The last 95 km follow the unpaved McCarthy Road to a footbridge, where cars must be left.
- **Walk or shuttle:** cross the footbridge on foot; a shuttle covers the remaining 8 km to Kennecott.
- Small air-taxi companies also fly to the airstrip at McCarthy.', 'Guided tours of the mill run in summer, from about late May to early September; the grounds are free to walk around, and there is a park visitor centre. Services are very limited. This is bear country, so follow the park''s safety advice.'),
  ('kitazawa-flotation-plant', 'The Kitazawa Flotation Plant stands in the town of Aikawa on Sado Island, in the Sea of Japan off Niigata. Sado was one of Japan''s most important sources of gold and silver for nearly four centuries, from the early 1600s until the last mine closed in 1989. In the late 1930s, as Japan prepared for war, a modern flotation plant was built at Kitazawa to process ore on a huge scale, said at the time to be the largest of its kind in East Asia. Its stepped concrete terraces climb the hillside, and since the plant closed they have slowly been taken over by trees and ivy, which has led people to compare them to the floating castle in a Studio Ghibli film. The plant is part of the Sado Island Gold Mines UNESCO World Heritage Site, inscribed in 2024, together with the historic mine tunnels nearby; the listing also prompted debate about Korean labourers who worked in the mines during the war.', '- **Plane:** Niigata Airport (KIJ) is the nearest airport; take a bus or taxi to Niigata port, about 25 minutes.
- **Ferry:** jetfoils reach Ryōtsu on Sado in about an hour, and car ferries in about 2½ hours.
- **Bus:** from Ryōtsu, buses reach Aikawa in about an hour.', 'The ruins can be seen freely from the paths around them, but the structures themselves cannot be entered. The historic mine tunnels nearby can be visited on tours with an entry fee. In the evening the ruins are lit up.'),
  ('kolmanskop', 'Kolmanskop is a ghost town in the Namib Desert, about 10 km inland from the port of Lüderitz in southern Namibia. In 1908, when Namibia was the German colony of South West Africa, a railway worker named Zacharias Lewala found a diamond in the sand here and showed it to his supervisor. A diamond rush followed, and the Germans built a prosperous town in the middle of the desert, with large stone villas, a ballroom, a theatre, a bowling alley, a school and a hospital with one of the first X-ray machines in the southern hemisphere. Fresh water had to be brought by train, and ice was made in the town''s own factory. After the First World War, diamond production fell and richer deposits were found further south, so people gradually left, and the town was abandoned by the 1950s. Since then the desert has moved in, and today rooms are knee-deep in sand.', '- **Car:** from Lüderitz Airport (LUD), about 10 km, the drive takes 15 minutes.
- **Plane:** Lüderitz has few flights; many visitors drive from Hosea Kutako International Airport (WDH) near Windhoek, about 700 km, a long day''s drive.', 'Kolmanskop lies in a restricted diamond area and can only be visited on guided tours, which leave in the morning; buy a permit at the gate. Photographers can buy a special permit for longer visits. Bring water and sun protection. Wind can blow sand hard in the afternoon.'),
  ('kuldhara', 'Kuldhara is an abandoned village in the Thar Desert, about 18 km west of Jaisalmer in Rajasthan, north-west India. It was founded centuries ago by Paliwal Brahmins, a community known for its skill in farming the dry land, and was one of a group of more than eighty Paliwal villages in the area. In the nineteenth century its inhabitants left, together with the people of the neighbouring villages, and never returned. Why they left is uncertain. Historians point to drought, heavy taxes and conflict with the rulers of Jaisalmer, while a popular legend says the villagers left in a single night to escape a cruel minister and cursed the village so that nobody could ever live there again. Today the roofless sandstone houses, laid out along straight streets, are protected as a heritage site, and a few houses and the temple have been restored.', '- **Plane:** Jaisalmer Airport (JSA), about 20 km away, has limited flights. Jodhpur (JDH), about 300 km away, has more.
- **Car or taxi:** about 30 minutes from Jaisalmer.', 'The village is open during the day with a small entry fee and closes at sunset. Summer is extremely hot, so the best time to visit is from October to March. A visit often combines with a night in a desert camp.'),
  ('landschaftspark-duisburg-nord', 'Landschaftspark Duisburg-Nord is a public park of about 180 hectares built around a former ironworks in the north of Duisburg. The Meiderich ironworks produced pig iron for more than eighty years until it closed in 1985. Rather than clear the site, planners kept the blast furnaces, gas tanks, ore bunkers and railway lines and let nature grow around and through them. Visitors can climb blast furnace 5 to a viewing platform about 70 metres up and look out over the Ruhr area, or walk through the old ore bunkers, which have been turned into gardens. A former gas tank is now a diving centre, and the old concrete walls are used for climbing. At night a coloured light installation turns the ironworks into a work of art. The park is one of the best-known examples of turning an industrial ruin into public space.', '- **Train and tram:** from Düsseldorf Airport (DUS), take a train to Duisburg Hauptbahnhof, about 15 minutes, then tram 903 to Landschaftspark Nord.
- **Car:** about 25 minutes from the airport.', 'The park is open all day and free to enter. Guided tours are available, including evening tours with the light installation. The climb up the blast furnace is on steep metal stairs; wear sturdy shoes.'),
  ('linnahall', 'Linnahall is a huge concrete and limestone building on the shore of Tallinn Bay, just outside the old town of Estonia''s capital. It was built as the V. I. Lenin Palace of Culture and Sport for the sailing events of the 1980 Moscow Olympics, which were held in Tallinn. Designed by the architects Raine Karp and Riina Altmäe, it is a low, stepped structure that rises from the waterfront like an ancient monument, with a concert hall for more than 4,000 people, an ice rink and a heliport. Concerts and events were held there until 2010, when it closed because of its poor condition. Since then the building has slowly decayed, with grass and graffiti on its broad terraces. Its roof and stairways remain open as a public promenade with views over the sea and the old town, while plans for its restoration are still being discussed.', '- **Tram or taxi:** from Tallinn Airport (TLL), about 5 km away, take the tram or a taxi to the city centre in about 15–20 minutes, then walk a few minutes from the old town to the harbour.', 'The building itself is closed, but the roof terraces and stairways are open as a public space at all times. The surfaces are uneven, slippery in winter and without railings in places. It is a popular spot at sunset.'),
  ('maunsell-sea-forts', 'The Maunsell Sea Forts are groups of steel towers standing on legs in the Thames Estuary, off the coasts of Kent and Essex. They were designed by the engineer Guy Maunsell and built in 1942 and 1943 to defend London against German bombers and the aircraft that dropped mines in the estuary. Each army fort consisted of seven towers linked by walkways, carrying guns, searchlights and living quarters for the soldiers. After the war the forts were abandoned, and in the 1960s some were taken over by pirate radio stations broadcasting pop music to Britain. Today the rusting towers at Red Sands and Shivering Sands look like giant creatures wading in the sea. A preservation group is working to restore Red Sands, and boat trips take visitors out to see the forts up close.', '- **Plane:** the nearest airport is London Southend (SEN). Most international travellers arrive at London Heathrow (LHR).
- **Train:** trains from London reach the Kent coast and the Thames ports in about 1–1½ hours.
- **Boat tour:** trips to the forts run in summer from harbours on the Kent coast and the Thames; departure points vary by operator.', 'The forts are normally seen from the water only; the preservation group occasionally organises visits onto Red Sands. Trips depend on weather and tides and can be cancelled at short notice. Bring warm, windproof clothing, even in summer.'),
  ('mos-espa-film-set', 'The Mos Espa film set stands among sand dunes in the desert of southern Tunisia, near the rock formation of Ong Jemel, about 30 km from the oasis town of Tozeur. It was built in 1997 for the film Star Wars: Episode I, The Phantom Menace, as the town on the desert planet Tatooine where the young Anakin Skywalker lives, and it was used again for a later film in the series. After filming, the crew left the set behind. Its domed houses, arches, towers and fake machinery, made from plaster and wood, still stand in the sand, weathered by the sun and wind. For years, moving dunes threatened to bury the set, and parts of it have been repaired over the years so that it can stay open to visitors. Today local traders sell souvenirs among the buildings, and the area attracts both film fans and tourists exploring the Sahara. Ong Jemel itself appeared in the film The English Patient.', '- **4x4 tour:** from Tozeur-Nefta International Airport (TOE), about 35 km, organised 4x4 trips take about an hour over desert tracks.
- **Plane:** Tozeur has few flights; most international visitors fly to Tunis-Carthage (TUN) and continue by domestic flight or a long drive.', 'The set is open to walk around, and local guides and traders are usually on site. It is very hot in summer and there is no shade, so go early in the morning or near sunset, and bring water. Do not drive into the dunes without a guide and a suitable vehicle.'),
  ('ohio-state-reformatory', 'The Ohio State Reformatory in Mansfield, Ohio, was built between 1886 and 1910 as a reformatory for young first-time offenders. Its massive administration building mixes several architectural styles, from Romanesque to Gothic, and looks more like a castle than a prison. Behind it stands one of the largest free-standing steel cellblocks in the world, six tiers high. The reformatory later became an ordinary prison, and conditions deteriorated until it closed in 1990 after a court ruling on overcrowding. Shortly afterwards it became famous as the main filming location of The Shawshank Redemption, released in 1994, and it has since appeared in many other films and music videos. Today a preservation society runs the building as a museum, with self-guided and guided tours of the cellblocks, the warden''s quarters and the chapel, as well as ghost hunts at night.', '- **Car:** from Cleveland Hopkins International Airport (CLE), about 115 km, allow 1¼ hours. There is no practical public transport.', 'The reformatory is open most days with an entry fee. Self-guided tours, guided tours and evening ghost walks are available. Parts of the building are unheated, so dress warmly in winter.'),
  ('okunoshima', 'Okunoshima is a small island in the Seto Inland Sea, off the coast of Hiroshima Prefecture in western Japan. From 1929 until 1945 it was the site of a secret factory where the Japanese army produced poison gas, including mustard gas, much of which was used in China. The island was removed from maps, and the workers, many of whom later suffered from long-term illnesses, were sworn to secrecy. After the war the factory was dismantled and the gas stocks were destroyed or dumped. Ruins of the power station, storage buildings and gun emplacements still stand among the trees, and a small Poison Gas Museum tells the island''s dark history and the story of its victims. Today Okunoshima is better known as "Rabbit Island", because of the hundreds of wild rabbits that roam freely and approach visitors, a strange contrast to the ruins.', '- **Bus and train:** from Hiroshima Airport (HIJ), take a bus or taxi to Mihara station, about 35 minutes, then a local train to Tadanoumi, about 25 minutes.
- **Ferry:** ferries from Tadanoumi reach Okunoshima in about 15 minutes.', 'The island is open to visitors, and there is a hotel. The museum charges a small entry fee. Follow the local rules on feeding the rabbits. Some ruins are unstable; do not enter fenced-off buildings.'),
  ('old-cahawba', 'Old Cahawba is the site of Alabama''s first permanent state capital, where the Alabama and Cahaba rivers meet in the centre of the state. The town was founded in 1819 and served as capital in the early 1820s, until the legislature moved away after repeated floods. Cahawba later revived as a busy river port for shipping cotton, and during the American Civil War a Confederate prison there held thousands of Union soldiers. After the war, floods, disease and the loss of the county seat drove people away, and by the early twentieth century Cahawba was a ghost town. Today it is an archaeological park where visitors walk along the old street grid past brick columns standing alone in the fields, the ruins of mansions, old cemeteries and an artesian well. Few buildings remain, but the landscape is evocative, and the site is known for its ghost stories.', '- **Car:** from Montgomery Regional Airport (MGM), about 80 km, allow 1¼ hours via Selma. There is no public transport.', 'The park is open daily; check the opening hours of the welcome centre. In summer it is hot and humid, with mosquitoes, so bring water and repellent. Flooding occasionally closes the access roads.'),
  ('olympic-village-elstal', 'The Olympic Village in Elstal, just west of Berlin, housed the male athletes of the 1936 Summer Olympics. Built by the German army, it consisted of more than a hundred low accommodation houses, a large dining hall for the nations, a swimming hall and a gymnasium, set in a landscaped park with a lake and birch woods. The American athlete Jesse Owens, who won four gold medals at the Games, stayed here, and his room has been restored. After the Olympics the village became an army training school, and from 1945 the Soviet army used it as a military camp until its troops left in 1992. Since then many buildings have decayed, while others have been renovated, and new apartments are being built on part of the site. A foundation looks after the historic core of the village and opens it to visitors.', '- **Train and bus:** regional trains run to Elstal. From Berlin Brandenburg Airport (BER) allow about 1–1¼ hours with one change, then take a local bus or walk about 30 minutes.
- **Car:** about 50 minutes from the airport.', 'The historic part of the village can be visited on guided tours from spring to autumn, mostly at weekends; book ahead. Some buildings are still in poor condition, so stay with the group and on the marked paths.'),
  ('oradour-sur-glane', 'Oradour-sur-Glane is a village in central France that has been left in ruins since 10 June 1944, when a unit of the Waffen-SS killed more than 640 of its inhabitants and set the village on fire. After the war, the French state decided that the ruins should be preserved as a memorial, and a new village was built beside them. Visitors walk along the original main street, past roofless houses, the remains of shops and workshops, rusting cars and sewing machines, and the tram tracks that once connected the village to Limoges. The church, where many of the women and children died, still stands as a burnt shell. An underground memorial centre, the Centre de la Mémoire, explains what happened and places it in the wider history of the war. Oradour is a place of remembrance rather than adventure, and it is visited quietly and respectfully by people from all over the world.', '- **Car or taxi:** Oradour is about 20 km from Limoges-Bellegarde Airport (LIG), around 30 minutes by road.
- **Bus:** buses from Limoges run a few times a day; check the timetable before you travel.
- **Train:** if you arrive from elsewhere in France, trains run to Limoges, for example from Paris in about 3½ hours.', 'Visitors enter the ruins through the Centre de la Mémoire. Silence is requested in the martyred village: stay on the paths and do not touch or move objects in the ruins. The site is suitable for all ages, but the exhibition describes the massacre in detail. Allow at least two hours.'),
  ('orford-ness', 'Orford Ness is a long shingle spit on the Suffolk coast in eastern England, separated from the mainland by the river Alde and Ore. For most of the twentieth century it was a secret military test site. Early radar experiments took place here in the 1930s, and from the 1950s the Atomic Weapons Research Establishment tested the components of nuclear bombs, without nuclear material, in concrete laboratories designed to contain accidental explosions. The most striking of them, with heavy concrete roofs raised on pillars, are known as "the pagodas". The military left in the 1980s, and in 1993 the National Trust bought the site. Today it is a nature reserve where rare shingle plants and birds live among crumbling bunkers, barracks and test cells, many of them deliberately left to decay. It is one of the strangest landscapes in Britain.', '- **Plane:** the nearest airport is Norwich (NWI), about 80 km away. Most international travellers arrive at London Stansted (STN), about two hours away.
- **Train and taxi:** trains run via Ipswich to Wickham Market or Melton; from there, take a taxi to Orford Quay, about 20 minutes.
- **Ferry:** the National Trust ferry crosses from Orford Quay to the spit.', 'The site is open on selected days, mainly from spring to autumn, and the number of visitors per day is limited, so book the ferry crossing in advance. The marked routes run for several kilometres over shingle: wear sturdy shoes, bring water and stay on the paths.'),
  ('ouvrage-hackenberg', 'The Ouvrage Hackenberg, near Veckring in Lorraine in north-eastern France, is the largest fortress of the Maginot Line, the chain of fortifications that France built along its border with Germany in the 1930s. Behind a ridge of hills, about 10 km of tunnels connect gun blocks, barracks, ammunition stores, a power station and kitchens, all buried deep underground. An electric railway carried ammunition and soldiers through the galleries, and around a thousand men could live inside. In 1940 the German army went around the Maginot Line instead of attacking it head-on, and in 1944 Hackenberg saw fighting during the American advance. Today a volunteer association maintains the fortress. Visitors ride the underground railway through the tunnels, see the machinery and gun turrets, and learn how life inside the fortress worked.', '- **Car:** from Luxembourg Airport (LUX), about 45 km, allow 45 minutes. There is no practical public transport to the fort.', 'The fortress can only be visited on guided tours, which run on set days, more often in summer. Allow about two hours. It is about 12 °C underground all year, so bring a jacket, and expect a fair amount of walking.'),
  ('paranapiacaba', 'Paranapiacaba is a small railway village on the edge of the Serra do Mar mountains, about 50 km south-east of São Paulo city. It was built in the second half of the nineteenth century by the British-owned São Paulo Railway, which ran the line carrying coffee from the São Paulo plateau down the steep escarpment to the port of Santos. Because the slope was too steep for ordinary trains, wagons were hauled up and down by cables and stationary steam engines, and the village housed the engineers and workers who ran them. Its wooden houses, straight streets and the station clock tower, often compared to Big Ben, give it a strangely English look, and it is often wrapped in thick fog. The cable system stopped working in the 1980s and the village declined, but it is now a protected heritage site. About a thousand people still live there, and a railway museum shows the old machinery.', '- **Plane:** São Paulo-Congonhas Airport (CGH) is about 50 km away; most international flights land at São Paulo-Guarulhos (GRU).
- **Train:** take the city train line to Rio Grande da Serra, about an hour from central São Paulo.
- **Bus:** from Rio Grande da Serra, a local bus reaches Paranapiacaba in about 20 minutes.', 'The village can be explored freely, and the railway museum charges a small entry fee. Weekends are busiest, with cafés and guided walks. Bring a rain jacket, as fog and drizzle are common. Trails into the rainforest around the village require a local guide.'),
  ('paris-catacombs', 'The Catacombs of Paris are a small part of a vast network of tunnels and quarries beneath the city, where limestone was dug out for centuries to build Paris. At the end of the eighteenth century, when the city''s overcrowded cemeteries had become a health hazard, the bones of several million Parisians were moved into the old quarries. Workers arranged skulls and thigh bones into walls and patterns and added plaques with poems and quotations about death. The official ossuary, about 1.5 km long and 20 metres below the streets, opened to visitors in the nineteenth century and is now run by the city as a museum. The much larger network of tunnels around it, explored by a secret community known as the "cataphiles", is closed to the public and illegal to enter.', '- **Plane:** Paris Orly (ORY) is the nearest airport, about 15 km away. Paris Charles de Gaulle (CDG) is the main international airport.
- **Train:** from Orly, take the Orlyval shuttle and the RER B to Denfert-Rochereau, where the entrance is, in about 35 minutes. From Charles de Gaulle, the RER B runs directly to Denfert-Rochereau in about 45 minutes.', 'Timed tickets must be booked online, often well in advance. The visit takes about an hour and involves 131 steps down and 112 steps up. It is about 14 °C underground all year. The visit is not suitable for people with heart or breathing problems or claustrophobia. Only the official route is open; entering other tunnels is illegal.'),
  ('pennhurst', 'Pennhurst State School and Hospital, near Spring City in south-eastern Pennsylvania, opened in 1908 as an institution for people with intellectual and physical disabilities. Built as a campus of brick buildings on a hillside, it grew far beyond its intended size and by the 1960s housed nearly 3,000 people in overcrowded wards. Conditions were often appalling, with widespread reports of neglect and abuse. In 1968 a television report, "Suffer the Little Children", exposed the conditions to the public, and a federal lawsuit filed in 1974 led to court rulings that helped establish the right of disabled people to live in their communities rather than in institutions. Pennhurst closed in 1987. Its empty buildings decayed for years, and part of the campus is now run as a seasonal haunted attraction, a use that disability advocates have criticised. A memorial and preservation group works to keep the real history alive.', '- **Car:** from Philadelphia International Airport (PHL), about 60 km, allow about an hour.', 'The site is private property. It can only be visited during the seasonal attraction in the autumn or on occasional history tours; at other times, do not enter. Thousands of people lived and suffered here, which is worth keeping in mind during a visit.'),
  ('pentedattilo', 'Pentedattilo is an abandoned village in the far south of Calabria, a few kilometres inland from the Ionian coast near Reggio Calabria. Its houses cling to the foot of a huge sandstone rock whose five pinnacles look like the fingers of a giant hand, which gave the village its Greek name, meaning "five fingers". The village is remembered for a bloody feud in 1686, when members of the ruling Alberti family were murdered by a rival baron. A major earthquake in 1783 damaged many buildings, and over the following centuries the inhabitants gradually moved to a new village closer to the sea, until the old one was completely empty by the 1960s. Since the 1980s volunteers and local associations have restored some of the houses, which now hold craft workshops and small exhibitions, and a short-film festival brings the village to life each summer.', '- **Car:** from Reggio Calabria Airport (REG), about 30 km, allow 35 minutes. The last part is a narrow mountain road. There is no public transport to the village.', 'The village is freely accessible. Some restored houses with craft shops are open, mainly in summer. The paths are steep and uneven, so wear good shoes. Please respect the restoration work and do not climb on the rock.'),
  ('petite-ceinture', 'The Petite Ceinture, or "little belt", is a railway line that once circled Paris inside the old city walls. Opened in stages from 1852, it carried goods and passengers between the city''s main stations and neighbourhoods, but the metro took away its passengers, and passenger trains stopped in 1934. Freight traffic slowly faded too, and by the 1990s most of the 32-kilometre ring had been abandoned. For years it was a hidden world of overgrown tracks, tunnels, graffiti and small gardens, explored by urban explorers and walkers even though it was off limits. The city of Paris has since opened several sections as public promenades and nature trails, where you can walk along the old tracks past former stations, some of them converted into cafés and venues. Other sections remain closed while the city decides on their future.', '- **Plane:** Paris Orly (ORY) is the nearest airport, about 15 km away. Paris Charles de Gaulle (CDG) is the main international airport.
- **Metro:** the open sections are spread around the city and are easy to reach by metro; check the City of Paris website for the current list of open sections and entrances.', 'The open sections are free and usually open during the day, with opening hours similar to the city''s parks. Closed sections and tunnels are off limits. Some former stations along the line are now bars and cultural venues that can be visited in the evening.'),
  ('petrova-gora-memorial', 'The Petrova Gora Memorial stands on the summit of Petrova Gora, a forested hill in central Croatia near the border with Bosnia and Herzegovina. It commemorates the people of the Kordun and Banija regions who died in the Second World War, many of them killed in 1942 when German and Ustaša forces tried to destroy the Partisan hospital and the civilians hiding in these woods. Designed by the sculptor Vojin Bakić and completed in 1981, the building is a tall, curving concrete structure that was clad in shining stainless steel, rising above the trees like an abstract sculpture. Inside were a museum, a library and a viewing platform. After the wars of the 1990s it was abandoned, and much of the steel cladding was stripped and sold, leaving the concrete exposed. Today it stands empty and weathered, one of the most powerful and melancholy of the Yugoslav memorials.', '- **Car:** from Zagreb Airport (ZAG), about 75 km, allow 1½ hours. The last part is a forest road. There is no public transport.', 'The grounds around the memorial are freely accessible, but the building is closed and unsafe; do not go inside, as debris can fall. The forest roads can be in poor condition, especially in winter.'),
  ('plokstine-missile-base', 'The Plokštinė missile base lies in the forests of Žemaitija National Park in western Lithuania. Built in secret by Soviet soldiers in the early 1960s, it was one of the first underground launch sites for nuclear ballistic missiles in the Soviet Union, with four silos for missiles that could reach targets across Western Europe. Local people were told nothing, and the base was hidden under earth mounds and trees. It was taken out of service in 1978 and later abandoned and looted. In 2012 it reopened as the Cold War Museum, where visitors walk through the underground corridors, control rooms and machine halls and can look down into one of the deep silos. Exhibitions tell the story of the arms race and of life in Soviet Lithuania, and the surrounding national park offers lakes and forest trails.', '- **Car:** from Palanga International Airport (PLQ), about 50 km, allow 50 minutes. Public transport is limited, with buses to the nearby village of Plateliai.', 'The museum is open all year with an entry fee, with fewer opening days in winter. Guided tours are available. It is cool underground, so bring a jacket.'),
  ('plymouth-montserrat', 'Plymouth was the capital of Montserrat, a small British Overseas Territory in the Caribbean, until the Soufrière Hills volcano awoke in 1995 after centuries of quiet. As the eruptions continued, the town and the southern part of the island were evacuated, and in 1997 pyroclastic flows and mudflows buried Plymouth under metres of ash and rock. Only the upper floors of some buildings, church towers and the outlines of streets and houses still stand out from the grey deposits. About two-thirds of Montserrat''s population left the island, and the south remains an exclusion zone where nobody may live. The volcano is monitored around the clock by the Montserrat Volcano Observatory. Visitors can see Plymouth from viewpoints in the north, such as Garibaldi Hill, or enter parts of the zone on authorised guided tours when the volcano is quiet. It is often called a modern Pompeii.', '- **Plane:** John A. Osborne Airport (MNI) on Montserrat has short flights from Antigua''s V. C. Bird International Airport (ANU), the main gateway.
- **Ferry:** on some days, a ferry runs from Antigua to Montserrat in about 1½ hours.
- **Car:** on the island, use a taxi or a local guide to reach the viewpoints.', 'The exclusion zone can only be entered on authorised tours, which depend on volcanic activity and can be cancelled at short notice. The viewpoints in the north are freely accessible. The observatory''s visitor centre explains the eruption and the monitoring.'),
  ('project-riese-osowka', 'Project Riese, German for "giant", was an enormous construction project started in 1943 in the Owl Mountains of Lower Silesia, in what is now south-western Poland. Beneath the mountains, Nazi Germany began digging a network of tunnels and halls whose purpose is still not fully known; theories range from an underground headquarters for Hitler to protected arms factories. The work was done by forced labourers and prisoners from a network of camps, and thousands of them died. When the war ended, the complexes were unfinished, and several of them have since been opened to visitors. Osówka is the largest of the accessible complexes, with almost 2 km of tunnels, half-finished halls and a water reservoir, together with a large concrete building above ground known as the "casino". Guided routes lead through the cold, damp galleries and explain both the engineering and the human cost of the project.', '- **Car:** from Wrocław Airport (WRO), about 90 km, allow 1½ hours.
- **Train and bus:** trains run from Wrocław to Wałbrzych in about 1¼ hours; local buses or taxis continue to Osówka.', 'Visits are on guided tours of different lengths; the longer routes include narrow passages and water. The tunnels are about 8 °C all year, so bring warm clothing and sturdy shoes. The complex was built with forced labour, and it is visited as a place of remembrance as well as engineering.'),
  ('prora', 'Prora is a gigantic seaside complex on the island of Rügen in the Baltic Sea, built between 1936 and 1939 as a holiday resort for the Nazi organisation "Strength through Joy". Eight identical six-storey blocks stretch for about 4.5 km along the beach, designed to give 20,000 people a holiday at the same time. Construction stopped when the Second World War began, and the resort never received a single holidaymaker. After the war the Soviet army and then the East German army used parts of it as barracks, while other parts were blown up or left as empty shells. For decades the endless concrete façades stood largely abandoned and became a famous lost place. Since the 2010s several blocks have been converted into apartments, hotels and a youth hostel, but stretches of the original ruin remain. A documentation centre explains the history of the complex and the ideology behind it.', '- **Plane:** the nearest airport is Rostock-Laage (RLG), about 160 km away, with few routes. Most international travellers fly to Berlin Brandenburg Airport (BER).
- **Train:** from Berlin, trains run to the island of Rügen in about 3½–4 hours, usually with a change in Stralsund; the line to Binz stops at Prora.
- **Car:** about two hours from Rostock-Laage, or 3½ hours from Berlin.', 'The beach promenade and the outside of the blocks are freely accessible. The documentation centre and a museum show the history of the complex and charge an entry fee. Ruined sections may be fenced off; do not enter them. In summer a visit combines easily with a day on the beach.'),
  ('pyramiden', 'Pyramiden is a former coal-mining settlement on Svalbard, named after the pyramid-shaped mountain above it. Founded by Sweden in 1910 and sold to the Soviet Union in 1927, it grew into a model Soviet town for around a thousand people, with a cultural palace, a sports hall, a heated swimming pool and what is often called the world''s northernmost statue of Lenin. Mining stopped in 1998 and the town was emptied within months, leaving buildings, furniture and books behind. The dry, cold climate has preserved much of it, and Pyramiden is now one of the best-kept Soviet ghost towns anywhere. A small hotel has reopened, and a few caretakers live there in season and guide visitors through the empty streets. Surrounded by glaciers and mountains, and reached only by boat or snowmobile, a visit to Pyramiden combines urban exploration with a real Arctic expedition.', '- **Plane:** fly to Svalbard Airport, Longyear (LYR), which has flights from Oslo (OSL) and Tromsø.
- **Boat tour (summer):** from about June to September, boat tours leave from Longyearbyen and take two to three hours each way, often passing a glacier on the way.
- **Snowmobile (late winter and spring):** guided snowmobile tours cross the frozen landscape to Pyramiden in a long day.
- There is no road to Pyramiden.', 'Visits are only possible on guided tours. Polar bears roam the area, so guides carry rifles and visitors may not wander off on their own. Most interiors are seen on short guided walks or from doorways. Dress for Arctic weather in every season. Tours usually allow two to three hours in the town, and overnight stays are possible at the hotel.'),
  ('real-de-catorce', 'Real de Catorce is an old silver-mining town high in the Sierra de Catorce in the state of San Luis Potosí, central Mexico, at about 2,700 metres above sea level. Founded in the late eighteenth century after rich silver deposits were found, it became one of the wealthiest mining towns of New Spain, with a large church, a mint and grand stone houses. When silver prices collapsed in the early twentieth century and the Mexican Revolution followed, the mines closed and the population fell from many thousands to a few hundred, leaving whole streets in ruins. The town is reached through the Ogarrio Tunnel, a 2.3 km single-lane former mining tunnel. Today Real de Catorce attracts pilgrims to its church of St Francis, tourists exploring the ruined mines and the ghost-town quarter, and the Wixárika people, for whom the desert below is sacred.', '- **Plane:** San Luis Potosí International Airport (SLP) is about 250 km away.
- **Bus:** take a bus to Matehuala, about three hours, then a local bus through the tunnel to Real de Catorce, about 1½ hours.
- **Car:** about 3½ hours from the airport. The last part is a cobbled road and the single-lane tunnel, with traffic alternating in each direction.', 'The town is open to visitors and lives from tourism. Jeep and horseback tours go to the ruined mines and the desert. Nights are cold at this altitude. Respect the sacred sites of the Wixárika people; collecting plants in the desert is forbidden.'),
  ('rhyolite', 'Rhyolite is a ghost town in the Nevada desert, just outside Death Valley National Park. After gold was discovered in the surrounding hills in 1904, a town of several thousand people sprang up almost overnight, with banks, a stock exchange, an opera house, three railway lines and concrete buildings that were meant to last. The boom was short: the mines produced less than hoped, the financial panic of 1907 dried up investment, and by 1920 the town was almost empty. Today the ruins include the shell of the three-storey Cook Bank building, the old school, the railway depot and a house built from tens of thousands of glass bottles. Next to the town is the Goldwell Open Air Museum, where ghostly sculptures, including a version of the Last Supper, stand in the desert. Rhyolite is often visited on the way into Death Valley.', '- **Car:** from Harry Reid International Airport (LAS) in Las Vegas, about 190 km, allow two hours via US Highway 95 to Beatty. Rhyolite lies about 6 km west of Beatty.', 'The ruins are freely accessible all year; some buildings are fenced off. In summer, temperatures can rise above 40 °C, so bring plenty of water. Watch out for rattlesnakes and old mine shafts, and stay on the tracks.'),
  ('ross-island-andaman', 'Ross Island, officially renamed Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose Dweep in 2018, is a small island near Port Blair in India''s Andaman and Nicobar Islands. From 1858 it was the administrative headquarters of the British penal settlement in the Andamans, where prisoners from the Indian independence movement were held. On this tiny island the British built a self-contained colonial town for officials and their families, with a church, a bakery, a ballroom, a club, a hospital and the Chief Commissioner''s residence, earning it the nickname "Paris of the East". An earthquake in 1941 damaged the buildings, and during the Second World War Japanese forces occupied the Andamans and built bunkers on the island. After the war it was abandoned. Today the ruins are wrapped in the roots of huge banyan and peepal trees, and spotted deer and peacocks wander among them.', '- **Taxi:** from Veer Savarkar International Airport (IXZ) in Port Blair, take a taxi to the jetty, about 15 minutes.
- **Ferry:** boats cross to the island in about 15 minutes.', 'The island is open during the day with an entry fee, and a light-and-sound show tells its history in the evening. It is hot and humid, so bring water. Please do not feed the deer.'),
  ('saint-pierre-ruins', 'Saint-Pierre, on the north-west coast of Martinique in the Caribbean, was once the island''s capital and its cultural and economic heart, known as the "Paris of the Caribbean". On the morning of 8 May 1902, the nearby volcano Mount Pelée erupted, and a cloud of superheated gas and ash swept down onto the town, destroying it within minutes and killing almost all of its roughly 28,000 inhabitants. One of the very few survivors was a prisoner, Ludger Sylbaris, protected by the thick walls of his cell, who later toured with a circus. The town was gradually rebuilt on a smaller scale, but many ruins were left standing among the new houses, including the theatre, the prison cell and the old warehouses of the Figuier district by the sea. Ships sunk during the eruption lie in the bay and are popular with divers. A memorial museum tells the story of the disaster.', '- **Car:** from Martinique Aimé Césaire International Airport (FDF), about 40 km, allow an hour.
- **Bus:** shared taxis run from Fort-de-France to Saint-Pierre.', 'The ruins in the town are freely accessible, and the memorial museum charges an entry fee. Mount Pelée is still active and closely monitored; hiking trails lead up the volcano. Saint-Pierre is a place where thousands died, so visit respectfully.'),
  ('salton-sea-bombay-beach', 'The Salton Sea is a large, salty lake in the desert of southern California, created by accident in 1905 when the Colorado River broke through irrigation canals and flooded a dry basin for nearly two years. In the 1950s and 1960s it became a fashionable resort area, with marinas, yacht clubs and holiday towns that drew huge numbers of visitors. But with no outlet, the lake grew saltier and was polluted by farm run-off, floods damaged shoreline buildings, and mass fish deaths left beaches covered in bones. Most visitors stopped coming, and towns such as Bombay Beach were left half empty, with ruined trailers and houses sinking into the mud. In recent years artists have moved to Bombay Beach, creating installations among the ruins and holding an annual art event, while the lake keeps shrinking and exposing dusty shores.', '- **Car:** from Palm Springs International Airport (PSP), about 90 km, allow 1¼ hours. There is no practical public transport.', 'The shore and the art installations are freely accessible. Summer temperatures can exceed 45 °C, so winter and spring are the best times to visit. The lake sometimes smells strongly. In Bombay Beach, respect residents'' property.'),
  ('sanatorio-duran', 'The Sanatorio Durán lies on the slopes of the Irazú volcano, about 2,300 metres above sea level, north of the city of Cartago in Costa Rica. It was founded in 1918 by the doctor and politician Carlos Durán Cartín as a sanatorium for tuberculosis patients, who were sent up to the cool, clean mountain air to recover. Over the years the complex grew into a large hospital with wards, a chapel and staff housing, treating hundreds of patients, many of them children. When antibiotics made sanatoriums unnecessary, the hospital closed in the 1970s, and after a period of other uses the buildings were abandoned. Their peeling paint, long corridors and graffiti have given the sanatorium a reputation as one of the most haunted places in Costa Rica, and it has appeared on television shows about the paranormal. Today the site is open to visitors for a small fee, with mist often drifting through the ruins.', '- **Car or taxi:** from Juan Santamaría International Airport (SJO), about 45 km, allow 1¼ hours via Cartago on the road towards the Irazú volcano.', 'The site is open during the day with a small entry fee. The buildings are unstable, so stay out of fenced areas. It is often cold and foggy at this altitude. A visit combines well with Irazú Volcano National Park further up the road.'),
  ('sarajevo-bobsleigh-track', 'The bobsleigh and luge track on Mount Trebević was built for the 1984 Winter Olympics in Sarajevo, then part of Yugoslavia, the first Winter Games held in a socialist country. The concrete track, about 1.3 km long with a series of banked curves, winds down through the pine forest above the city. During the siege of Sarajevo in the 1990s the mountain lay on the front line: Bosnian Serb forces used the track as an artillery position, and the surrounding forest was mined. After the war the track was left to decay. Today it is covered with layers of colourful graffiti and street art, and walking along its curves among the trees is one of the most popular outings from Sarajevo. Since the Trebević cable car was rebuilt in 2018, the mountain is once again easy to reach from the old town.', '- **Taxi:** from Sarajevo International Airport (SJJ), about 15 km away, take a taxi into the city, about 20 minutes, or directly up the mountain, about 30 minutes.
- **Cable car and walk:** the cable car from the old town reaches the top of Trebević in about 10 minutes; from there, walk about 20 minutes to the track.', 'The track is freely accessible. Landmines have been cleared from the area, but stay on the track and on well-used paths. The concrete can be slippery after rain, and there is snow on the mountain in winter.'),
  ('shuinandong-smelter', 'The Shuinandong Smelter, popularly known as the "Thirteen Levels", is a huge ruined industrial building terraced into the mountainside above the sea at Ruifang, on Taiwan''s north-east coast. It was built by a Japanese mining company in the 1930s, when Taiwan was under Japanese rule, to refine ore from the gold and copper mines of nearby Jinguashi. After the war it was taken over by a Taiwanese state mining company and closed in the 1980s. Its stepped grey walls look like a ruined fortress from a fantasy film, and long abandoned exhaust pipelines climb the mountain behind it. Below, the water of the so-called Yin-Yang Sea is coloured yellow by minerals washing down from the hills. The building itself is closed, but it can be seen from the coastal road and nearby viewpoints, and on some evenings it is lit up with coloured lights.', '- **Plane:** Taipei Songshan Airport (TSA) is about 35 km away; Taiwan Taoyuan International (TPE) is the main international airport.
- **Train and bus:** take a train from Taipei to Ruifang, about 45 minutes, then a bus towards Jinguashi and Shuinandong, about 30 minutes.', 'The smelter can only be seen from outside, from the road and viewpoints. A visit combines well with the Gold Museum in Jinguashi and the old mountain town of Jiufen.'),
  ('slievemore-deserted-village', 'The Deserted Village at Slievemore lies on the southern slope of Slievemore mountain on Achill Island, off the west coast of Ireland in County Mayo. Between 80 and 100 stone cottages stand in a long line beside an old track, their roofs long gone, with traces of the small fields their inhabitants once farmed. The village was largely abandoned in the nineteenth century, around the time of the Great Famine, when many families moved to the coast, emigrated or were evicted. Afterwards some families used the houses seasonally as "booley" homes when they brought their cattle up to summer pastures, a practice that continued into the twentieth century. Today the ruins are protected, and archaeologists study them at a field school each summer. With the Atlantic on one side and the bare mountain on the other, it is a hauntingly beautiful place.', '- **Car:** from Ireland West Airport Knock (NOC), about 110 km, allow 1½–2 hours. Achill Island is connected to the mainland by a bridge.
- **Bus:** buses run from Westport to Achill; from the bus stop you will need a taxi or a long walk to the village.', 'The village is freely accessible all year. Park near the cemetery at the eastern end and walk along the track. The ground is uneven and can be boggy, so wear walking boots. Please do not climb on the walls of the ruins.'),
  ('sloss-furnaces', 'Sloss Furnaces is a former blast-furnace plant on the edge of downtown Birmingham, Alabama, a city that grew up around iron and steel. The company was founded in 1881 by James Withers Sloss, and its furnaces produced pig iron for about ninety years, until 1971. Much of the hard and dangerous work was done by Black workers, often for low wages and under segregation, and the museum today tells their story along with that of the industry. After it closed, the plant was saved from demolition and became a National Historic Landmark, one of very few blast-furnace plants in the United States preserved as a historic site. Visitors can walk among the towering furnaces, boilers, stoves and pipes. Sloss is also a centre for metal arts, with workshops and public iron pours, and it hosts concerts, festivals and a popular Halloween haunted house.', '- **Taxi:** from Birmingham-Shuttlesworth International Airport (BHM), about 5 km, allow 10 minutes.', 'The grounds are open to visitors several days a week; check the opening days. Guided tours are available. It can be very hot in summer, and there is little shade among the furnaces.'),
  ('solheimasandur-plane-wreck', 'On 21 November 1973 a US Navy Douglas DC-3 transport plane ran into trouble in bad weather and made an emergency landing on Sólheimasandur, a vast black sand plain on the south coast of Iceland. Everyone on board survived. The aircraft was too badly damaged to fly again, so the Navy removed what it could use and left the fuselage where it had landed. Over the years wind, sand and sea air stripped its paint, and the silver wreck lying alone on the black sand became one of Iceland''s most photographed sights, appearing in music videos and advertisements. Reaching it means a walk of about 4 km each way across a flat, featureless plain from a car park beside the Ring Road. The landscape is bleak and dramatic, especially in the low light of winter or under the midnight sun of summer.', '- **Car:** from Keflavík International Airport (KEF), about 170 km, allow 2½ hours along the Ring Road (Route 1) to the car park.
- **Walk or shuttle:** from the car park, walk about 4 km each way, around 45–60 minutes, or take the shuttle bus.', 'The wreck is freely accessible; there is a parking fee. The walk is flat but exposed, and strong winds and sandstorms can arrive quickly, so check the weather and follow the marked path. Please do not climb on the wreck. Driving onto the sand is forbidden.'),
  ('spinalonga', 'Spinalonga is a small rocky island in the Gulf of Elounda on the north-east coast of Crete. The Venetians built a powerful sea fortress there in the sixteenth century to protect the harbour, and it held out against the Ottomans long after the rest of Crete had fallen. In 1903 the island became a colony for people with leprosy, who were sent there from across Crete and later from all of Greece. The patients lived in the old houses inside the walls, with a church, shops and a hospital, cut off from the world, until effective treatment arrived and the colony closed in 1957, making it one of the last leper colonies in Europe. The abandoned island became widely known through Victoria Hislop''s novel The Island. Today visitors walk through the fortress gate and along the main street, past ruined houses, the disinfection building and the cemetery.', '- **Bus or car:** from Heraklion International Airport (HER), about 65 km, travel to Elounda or Plaka, around an hour by car.
- **Ferry:** frequent boats cross to the island from Plaka in about 10 minutes and from Elounda in about 20 minutes. Excursion boats also run from Agios Nikolaos.', 'The island is open daily with an entry fee. There is little shade, so bring water and sun protection in summer. Spinalonga was home to people who suffered long isolation; please visit with respect.'),
  ('spreepark', 'Spreepark opened in 1969 as the Kulturpark Plänterwald, the only permanent amusement park in East Germany, on the banks of the river Spree in south-east Berlin. After reunification a private operator took it over, renamed it Spreepark and added new rides, but visitor numbers fell and the park closed in the early 2000s, deep in debt. For almost twenty years its rusting roller coaster, toppled fibreglass dinosaurs, swan-shaped boats and large Ferris wheel slowly disappeared into the undergrowth, and the park became a magnet for urban explorers, film crews and photographers. The city of Berlin bought the site back in 2014 and is turning it into a new kind of park that combines art, nature and some of the old attractions, including the restored Ferris wheel. The reopening is planned for spring 2027, which will end the park''s long life as one of Berlin''s best-known abandoned places.', '- **Train and walk:** from Berlin Brandenburg Airport (BER), take the S-Bahn to Plänterwald or Treptower Park, about 30 minutes, then walk 10–15 minutes through the Plänterwald forest.
- **Taxi:** about 25 minutes from the airport.', 'The park is closed while it is being rebuilt. Check the park''s official channels for any guided previews or events before the planned reopening in spring 2027. Do not climb the fences: the site is an active construction area.'),
  ('st-elmo', 'St. Elmo is a former gold- and silver-mining town in the Sawatch Range of the Rocky Mountains in Colorado, at about 3,000 metres above sea level. Founded in 1880, it grew to around two thousand people, with hotels, saloons, a dance hall and a newspaper, and it was a stop on a railway line that tunnelled under the Continental Divide. When the mines declined and the railway closed in the 1920s, people moved away, and St. Elmo became a ghost town. Unusually, many of its wooden buildings along the main street still stand, including the general store, the town hall and the schoolhouse. Some are privately owned, and the general store opens as a shop in summer. The town is also known for the chipmunks that dart along its boardwalks. Surrounded by forest and high peaks, St. Elmo is a popular starting point for off-road trails.', '- **Plane:** the nearest airport is Gunnison-Crested Butte (GUC), about 110 km away. Denver International Airport (DEN), about 240 km away, has many more flights.
- **Car:** from Denver, allow about 3½ hours. The last 20 km is a gravel road, which can be snowy in winter.', 'The town is freely accessible, but most buildings are private and can only be seen from outside. The general store is open in summer. At this altitude, take it easy and be ready for sudden changes in the weather.'),
  ('st-kilda', 'St Kilda is a group of islands in the Atlantic, about 65 km west of the Outer Hebrides, and the most remote part of the British Isles. People lived on the main island, Hirta, for thousands of years, surviving on seabirds, sheep and fishing. In the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, contact with the outside world, disease and emigration weakened the small community, and in 1930 the last 36 inhabitants asked to be evacuated to the mainland. Their village still stands: a curving street of stone cottages, a church and school, and more than a thousand small stone storage huts called cleits scattered across the hillsides. St Kilda is owned by the National Trust for Scotland and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site for both its nature and its culture, with huge colonies of gannets, puffins and fulmars on the highest sea cliffs in Britain.', '- **Plane:** fly to Stornoway (SYY) on the Isle of Lewis, from Glasgow (GLA) or Edinburgh.
- **Car:** drive about 1½–2 hours south to Leverburgh on the Isle of Harris.
- **Boat tour:** day trips from Leverburgh take about 2½–3 hours each way, weather permitting, from about April to September.', 'Day trips give about four to five hours ashore on Hirta. Landings are often cancelled because of the weather, so plan spare days. A National Trust ranger meets visitors, and a small museum is housed in one of the cottages. Keep away from cliff edges and nesting seabirds.'),
  ('teufelsberg', 'Teufelsberg is an artificial hill in Berlin''s Grunewald forest, piled up after the Second World War from the rubble of the bombed city. On its summit stand the remains of a Cold War listening station, run by the United States and the United Kingdom to intercept radio traffic from East Germany and the wider Eastern Bloc. Its white radomes, the domes that once sheltered the antennas, can still be seen from across western Berlin. When the Allies left in the early 1990s, investors planned hotels and flats on the hill, but the projects failed and the station was left to decay. Today the site is privately run and open to visitors for an entry fee. Its walls and towers carry large-scale graffiti and street art, making it one of Berlin''s best-known urban exploration sites. From the top of the main tower there are wide views over the forest and the city.', '- **Train and walk:** from Berlin Brandenburg Airport (BER), take a regional train or S-Bahn into the city and change to the S-Bahn towards Spandau, getting off at Heerstraße. From there it is a 25–30 minute walk uphill through the forest. Allow about 1¼ hours in total.
- **Car or taxi:** about 45 minutes from the airport. Park at the edge of the forest and walk the last part.', 'The station is open to visitors most days, with an entry fee, and guided tours explain its history. The site is uneven and partly unfinished: wear sturdy shoes and stay within the marked areas. The top of the radome tower is exposed and can be windy and cold. Allow two to three hours.'),
  ('tjentiste-war-memorial', 'The Tjentište War Memorial stands in the valley of the Sutjeska river in Sutjeska National Park, in south-eastern Bosnia and Herzegovina. It commemorates the Battle of the Sutjeska in 1943, when Yugoslav Partisans led by Josip Broz Tito broke out of an encirclement by German and Italian forces at the cost of thousands of lives. Completed in 1971 and designed by the sculptor Miodrag Živković, the memorial consists of two huge, fractured concrete forms facing each other, said to represent the breakthrough. It is one of the best known of the Yugoslav memorials called spomeniks, abstract monuments built across the country in the decades after the war. A memorial hall with large frescoes and a cemetery for the fallen lie nearby. Set against forested mountains, the site is both a place of remembrance and a striking piece of modernist sculpture.', '- **Car:** from Sarajevo International Airport (SJJ), about 110 km, allow two hours via Foča.
- **Bus:** some buses between Sarajevo and the south of the country pass Tjentište; check timetables before you travel.', 'The memorial is freely accessible all year; the memorial hall''s opening hours vary. It is a war memorial and should be visited respectfully. A visit combines well with hiking in Sutjeska National Park.'),
  ('trans-allegheny-lunatic-asylum', 'The Trans-Allegheny Lunatic Asylum in Weston, West Virginia, is one of the largest hand-cut sandstone buildings in North America. It was built between 1858 and 1881 following the ideas of the psychiatrist Thomas Kirkbride, who believed that a calm, spacious building with plenty of light and fresh air could help patients recover. Designed for about 250 patients, it held more than 2,000 by the 1950s, and overcrowding, poor conditions and harsh treatments replaced the original ideals. The hospital, later known as Weston State Hospital, closed in 1994. Its long main building, with a clock tower at the centre and wings stretching out on both sides, stood empty for years. Since the late 2000s private owners have opened it for tours that explain the history of mental health care, along with popular paranormal tours at night.', '- **Plane:** the nearest airport is North Central West Virginia (CKB), about 45 km away. Pittsburgh International (PIT), about 200 km away, has many more flights.
- **Car:** about 40 minutes from CKB, or 2½ hours from Pittsburgh.', 'Visits are on guided tours only, ranging from history tours to night-time paranormal tours; book ahead. Some tours are long and involve many stairs. Remember that this was a hospital where many people suffered.'),
  ('tskaltubo-sanatoriums', 'Tskaltubo is a spa town in western Georgia, close to the city of Kutaisi, known for its warm, mildly radioactive mineral springs. In Soviet times it was one of the most popular health resorts in the Soviet Union, with direct trains from Moscow and around twenty sanatoriums built in a grand neoclassical style around a large park, together with ornate bathhouses where patients took the waters. Joseph Stalin had his own private bath here. After the collapse of the Soviet Union the visitors disappeared, and from the 1990s many sanatoriums housed people displaced by the war in Abkhazia, some of whom still live there. Others were left to decay, their columned halls, mosaics and staircases slowly taken over by plants. In recent years some buildings have been restored as hotels, and the town is trying to revive its spa tradition, but many of the old sanatoriums still stand empty.', '- **Taxi:** from Kutaisi International Airport (KUT), about 15 km, allow 20 minutes.
- **Bus:** minibuses (marshrutkas) run between Kutaisi and Tskaltubo.', 'Many sanatoriums can be seen from the street and the park. Some are inhabited, so respect residents'' privacy and ask before entering; others are guarded or fenced, and floors and stairs in empty buildings can be unsafe. Some bathhouses still operate, and you can take a bath in the mineral water.'),
  ('tyneham', 'Tyneham is a small village in a valley near the Dorset coast in southern England. In December 1943 its 225 residents left their homes so that the army could use the land for training, believing they would return after the war. They never did: the area became part of the Lulworth army ranges and is still used for gunnery practice. The village has slowly fallen into ruin, but the church and the schoolhouse have been restored and turned into small exhibitions about life in Tyneham, with the pupils'' names still above the coat hooks. The roofless cottages, the old telephone box and the farm buildings give a quiet, melancholy picture of rural England frozen in time. A path leads from the village down to Worbarrow Bay, one of the most beautiful stretches of the Jurassic Coast.', '- **Car:** from Bournemouth Airport (BOH), about 45 km, allow an hour. There is no public transport to the village. From London Heathrow (LHR) allow about three hours.', 'The village and the range walks are open on most weekends and during school holidays, when there is no firing; check the Lulworth Ranges opening times before you go. Entry to the village is free. Stay on the marked paths: the ranges may contain unexploded shells.'),
  ('varosha', 'Varosha, known in Turkish as Maraş, is the southern quarter of the city of Famagusta on the east coast of Cyprus. In the early 1970s it was one of the most popular beach resorts in the Mediterranean, with high-rise hotels, restaurants and shops along a long sandy beach. During the 1974 conflict, when Turkish forces took control of the north of the island, its Greek Cypriot residents fled, and the district was fenced off. For more than 45 years nobody was allowed in, and Varosha became a famous ghost town of crumbling hotels, empty streets and abandoned shops. In 2020 the Turkish Cypriot authorities opened some streets and the beach to visitors, a step criticised by the United Nations and the Republic of Cyprus, and hundreds of thousands of people have since walked or cycled through. Most buildings remain closed and unsafe, and Varosha''s future is still part of the unresolved Cyprus dispute.', '- **Car or taxi:** from Larnaca International Airport (LCA), about 55 km, allow about an hour to Famagusta. You cross the line between the two parts of the island at a crossing point, so bring your passport.', 'The opened area can be visited during the day, and bicycles and electric carts can be hired. Only the designated streets are open; the buildings are closed and dangerous. Photography of military areas is forbidden. Check your government''s travel advice about crossing between the two parts of Cyprus.'),
  ('villa-epecuen', 'Villa Epecuén was a spa town on the shore of Lake Epecuén, near the town of Carhué in the west of Buenos Aires Province, about 550 km from the city of Buenos Aires. The lake''s water is extremely salty, and from the 1920s visitors came from all over Argentina to bathe in it, believing it could heal skin and joint problems. The small town had hotels, guesthouses, restaurants and shops, and in summer thousands of tourists filled its streets. After years of unusually heavy rain, the lake kept rising, and in November 1985 the embankment protecting the town broke. Water flooded the streets, and within years the town lay several metres under the lake. From around 2009 the water slowly drew back, revealing streets of ruined buildings and dead trees, all bleached white by salt. Pablo Novak, who lived alone near the ruins for decades until his death in 2022, became famous as the town''s last resident.', '- **Car:** from Comandante Espora Airport (BHI) in Bahía Blanca, about 250 km, allow three hours; from Buenos Aires-Ezeiza (EZE) the drive takes about seven hours.
- **Bus:** long-distance buses run from Buenos Aires to Carhué; from Carhué the ruins are about 10 km away by taxi or bicycle.', 'The ruins are open and free to explore on foot. There is little shade and the salt reflects the sun strongly, so bring sun protection and water. A small museum in Carhué tells the town''s story. Do not climb on the unstable walls.'),
  ('volklingen-ironworks', 'The Völklingen Ironworks in Saarland, near the French border, is the only fully preserved integrated ironworks from the great age of iron production in Western Europe and North America. Founded in the 1870s, it grew into one of Europe''s leading iron producers, with six blast furnaces, a vast blowing-engine hall and a web of conveyors, bunkers and walkways. Production stopped in 1986, and only eight years later, in 1994, the site was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Today visitors can follow the route of the ore from the bunkers to the top of the blast furnaces, walk through the cavernous blower hall and explore the "Paradise", a garden where a forest has grown over the old coking plant. The ironworks is also a major venue for large art and culture exhibitions, which fill its industrial halls.', '- **Plane:** the nearest airport is Saarbrücken (SCN), about 25 km away, with few routes. Most international travellers fly to Frankfurt (FRA) and take the train, about 2½ hours.
- **Train:** trains from Saarbrücken Hauptbahnhof reach Völklingen in about 10 minutes.
- **Walk:** the entrance is about five minutes from Völklingen station.', 'The ironworks is open daily, with an entry fee that includes the current exhibition. Allow at least half a day. The walkways and stairs up the blast furnaces are steep, and the site can be hot in summer; wear sturdy shoes and bring water.'),
  ('waverly-hills-sanatorium', 'Waverly Hills Sanatorium stands on a wooded hill on the south-western edge of Louisville, Kentucky. It opened as a small tuberculosis hospital in 1910, when the disease was spreading fast in the area, and in 1926 a large new five-storey building replaced it, able to treat around 400 patients. Treatment relied on rest, fresh air and sunlight, with patients wheeled out onto long open porches in all weathers. Before antibiotics, many did not recover, and the hospital used a tunnel down the hill, later nicknamed the "body chute", to take away the bodies of those who died out of sight of the other patients. After new drugs made the sanatorium unnecessary, it closed in 1961 and later served as a care home until the early 1980s. Abandoned and vandalised for years, it gained a reputation as one of the most haunted places in the United States. Today its owners run history and paranormal tours.', '- **Taxi or car:** from Louisville Muhammad Ali International Airport (SDF), about 15 km, allow 20 minutes.', 'Visits are on guided tours only, from history tours to overnight paranormal investigations; book ahead. The tours involve many stairs. Many patients died here, so please visit respectfully.'),
  ('whalers-bay', 'Whalers Bay lies inside Deception Island, in the South Shetland Islands off the Antarctic Peninsula. The island is the rim of an active volcano whose crater has been flooded by the sea, and ships enter it through a narrow gap called Neptune''s Bellows. From 1912 to 1931 a Norwegian company ran a whaling station on the black volcanic beach, boiling whale blubber into oil in huge iron tanks. In 1928 the Australian explorer Hubert Wilkins made the first powered flight in Antarctica from the island. A British research base was later set up at the old station, but volcanic eruptions in 1967 and 1969 sent mud and ash through the buildings, and the base was abandoned. Today the rusting tanks and boilers, collapsed buildings, an aircraft hangar and a small cemetery lie along the steaming shore. Whalers Bay is a protected historic site under the Antarctic Treaty, and visitors must follow strict rules.', '- **Plane and cruise:** there is no airport. Most visitors arrive on expedition cruises that start in Ushuaia, Argentina (USH); crossing the Drake Passage takes about two days.
- **Fly and cruise:** some cruises start with a flight from Punta Arenas in Chile to King George Island, then continue by ship.', 'Passengers go ashore by inflatable boat during the Antarctic summer, from November to March. Do not touch or enter the historic structures, and keep a safe distance from seals and penguins. The water near the shore is warmed by the volcano, and some cruises let passengers take a short swim.'),
  ('wolfs-lair', 'The Wolf''s Lair, Wilczy Szaniec in Polish and Wolfsschanze in German, was Adolf Hitler''s main military headquarters on the Eastern Front during the Second World War. Built in a forest near Kętrzyn in the Masurian lake district, then part of East Prussia, the complex contained around 200 buildings, including massive concrete bunkers with walls several metres thick, barracks, a railway station and an airfield, all camouflaged from the air. Hitler spent more than 800 days here between 1941 and 1944. It was here, on 20 July 1944, that a bomb planted by Claus von Stauffenberg exploded in a failed attempt to kill him. As the Red Army approached in January 1945, German engineers blew up the bunkers, but many were too strong to destroy completely. Today huge broken slabs of concrete lie scattered among the trees, and the site is an outdoor museum.', '- **Plane:** the nearest airport is Olsztyn-Mazury (SZY), about 100 km away. Gdańsk (GDN), about 200 km away, has more international flights.
- **Train and bus:** trains run to Kętrzyn; from there it is about 8 km to the site by local bus or taxi.
- **Car:** about 1¼ hours from Olsztyn-Mazury Airport.', 'The site is open daily with an entry fee, and guided tours and audio guides are available. Stay on the marked paths and do not climb on the bunkers, which are unstable. In summer, bring insect repellent. Treat the site as a historical memorial.'),
  ('wunsdorf', 'Wünsdorf, a small town about 40 km south of Berlin, was for most of the twentieth century one of the largest military sites in Germany. The German army built barracks and training grounds here before the First World War, and in the 1930s the Wehrmacht''s high command dug a network of bunkers known as Maybach I and II, together with the Zeppelin communications centre. From the 1950s until 1994 Wünsdorf was the headquarters of the Soviet, later Russian, forces in Germany. Tens of thousands of soldiers and their families lived behind its walls, in a town that East Germans were not allowed to enter, which earned it the name "the Forbidden City". When the troops left, they left behind officers'' houses, a theatre, sports halls, Lenin statues and Soviet murals. Parts of the area have since become housing and a "book town" with second-hand bookshops, while many buildings still stand empty.', '- **Train:** regional trains run from Berlin to Wünsdorf-Waldstadt. From Berlin Brandenburg Airport (BER) allow about an hour with one change.
- **Car:** about 35 minutes from the airport.', 'Guided tours of the bunkers and the former garrison start from the visitor centre in the book town. They run regularly in season and should be booked ahead. The bunkers are cold all year, so bring a jacket. Many empty buildings are fenced off; do not enter them on your own.'),
  ('zollverein', 'The Zollverein Coal Mine Industrial Complex in Essen was once one of the largest coal mines in Europe. Its Shaft XII, opened in 1932, was designed in a clean, Bauhaus-inspired style by the architects Fritz Schupp and Martin Kremmer and became known as "the most beautiful coal mine in the world". The mine closed in 1986 and the coking plant next to it in 1993, ending more than 130 years of mining on the site. Instead of being demolished, the buildings were preserved, and in 2001 Zollverein was added to the UNESCO World Heritage list. Today the huge brick halls, conveyor bridges and coking ovens house the Ruhr Museum, design exhibitions, restaurants and event spaces, while the grounds are open to walk around freely. Zollverein has become the symbol of the Ruhr region''s change from heavy industry to culture.', '- **Train and tram:** from Düsseldorf Airport (DUS), take a train to Essen Hauptbahnhof, about 25 minutes, then tram 107 to Zollverein, about 15 minutes.
- **Car:** about 30 minutes from the airport.', 'The grounds are open all day and free to walk around. The museums and guided tours of the coal washery and coking plant charge entry fees. In summer there is an open-air swimming pool at the coking plant, and in winter an ice rink. Allow at least half a day.');





